window.SEARCH_INDEX=[{"u":"","p":"Copilot Flight Deck","h":"","t":"","x":"Unofficial · for people who use Copilot at work Get everything out of the Copilot you've got. Copilot is the AI your workplace gives you. This is how to make it pull its weight: the best route for your licence, prompts you fill in and copy, and what to check before you trust the answer. Search"},{"u":"","p":"Copilot Flight Deck","h":"First: which Copilot do you have?","t":"","x":"First: which Copilot do you have? Go to copilot.cloud.microsoft, sign in with your work account and read the label under your name. Copilot Chat (Basic) or M365 Copilot (Basic): follow the Chat sections on each page. M365 Copilot (Premium): follow the Premium sections. Start here walks you through it."},{"u":"#jobs","p":"Copilot Flight Deck","h":"What do you want to get done?","t":"","x":"What do you want to get done? Clear my inbox Triage unread mail, draft replies, flag what can wait. Write a first draft From rough notes to a structured draft you can edit. Shorten or sharpen Make existing text shorter, clearer or better pitched. Summarise a long document Get the point, the risks and the gaps from a long file. Prepare for a meeting Agenda, questions and likely objections in five minutes. Follow up a meeting Decisions, actions and owners, sent while it is fresh. Make sense of a spreadsheet Trends, outliers and formulas without breaking the file. Build a deck A structured first deck from a prompt or a document. Research a topic A sourced answer you can check, not a confident guess. Plan a piece of work Steps, risks and a first week, pressure-tested. Learn something fast A personal tutor for a topic, a tool or a piece of jargon. Catch up after time away Back from leave: what happened, what is waiting on you."},{"u":"#tools","p":"Copilot Flight Deck","h":"Tools","t":"","x":"Tools Prompt library Every prompt on the site. Search it, fill in the blanks, copy. Can my Copilot do this? Look up any feature against your licence. Power features Pages, Notebooks, memory and the other features most people never find. The 12 moves The habits that turn an average answer into a useful one. 5-day challenge Ten minutes a day. Ticks are saved in your browser. The Copilot way Heard what other AI tools do? Here is the nearest route in Copilot. Fix it Missing buttons, wrong account, empty answers."},{"u":"#tracks","p":"Copilot Flight Deck","h":"Everything for your licence","t":"","x":"Everything for your licence Copilot Chat Label: Copilot Chat (Basic) or M365 Copilot (Basic). What your licence can do Copilot Chat Word and Outlook · Excel · PowerPoint · Teams Agents · make your own One-page cheat sheet (print) M365 Copilot (Premium) Label: M365 Copilot (Premium). What your licence can do Copilot Chat and work data Word and Outlook · Excel · PowerPoint · Teams Agents · make your own One-page cheat sheet (print)"},{"u":"#how","p":"Copilot Flight Deck","h":"How this site works","t":"","x":"How this site works You are the pilot. Copilot drafts, searches and summarises. You decide, check and send. Facts come from Microsoft's own pages. Each guide says when it was last checked, and says so where something could not be confirmed. Nothing here needs admin rights. Where only an administrator can change something, the guide says so and shows how to check your own situation. Read Safety and privacy before you paste anything sensitive."},{"u":"start/","p":"Start here","h":"","t":"","x":"Quickstart · 2 minutes Start here Three steps. You finish knowing what you have and with one prompt that gives you something useful."},{"u":"start/","p":"Start here","h":"The short version","t":"","x":"The short version Go to copilot.cloud.microsoft and sign in with your work account, not a personal one. Read the label under your name. It tells you which track to follow: Chat or Premium. Give Copilot a goal, who it is for, and the format you want. Then refine it. Sign in with your work account Open copilot.cloud.microsoft. Use the email address your employer gave you. In the work version you should see the word Work under your profile and a green data protection shield. Personal accounts look different and have separate chats, files and protections. Read your label Under your name you will see one of three labels: Copilot Chat (Basic), M365 Copilot (Basic) or M365 Copilot (Premium). The first two are the Chat track on this site. The third is the Premium track. If you see no label, use Microsoft's licence details page or see Troubleshooting. Send one good prompt Pick the prompt for your track below, change the brackets, and send it. If the first answer is not right, say what to change. Refining is normal."},{"u":"start/#first-prompt","p":"Start here","h":"Your first prompt","t":"","x":"Your first prompt Microsoft says a prompt has four parts: a goal (the only required one), context, a source and your expectations. This one uses three of them and works on both tracks. Prompt I need to explain [topic or decision] to [who: my manager / a client / my team]. They know [what they already know] and care about [what they care about]. Write a first draft in under 150 words, in a friendly and direct tone. Then list the three questions they are most likely to ask."},{"u":"start/#chat","p":"Start here","h":"Next, on the Chat track","t":"chat","x":"Next, on the Chat track Copilot does not go looking through your files for you. To use a document, upload it or select it with your prompt. See giving it your files. Prompt · Chat track Using only the document I have attached, summarise it in five bullet points for [who]. If something is not in the document, say so instead of guessing. Pick a job from the playbooks What your licence can do Copilot Chat in depth"},{"u":"start/#premium","p":"Start here","h":"Next, on the Premium track","t":"premium","x":"Next, on the Premium track Copilot can search your emails, files and meetings for you, within what you already have permission to open. You can name a file instead of uploading it. Prompt · Premium track Look at my emails and meetings from the last week about [project]. Summarise what has been decided, what is still open, and anything waiting on me. Name the source of each point. Pick a job from the playbooks What your licence can do Copilot Chat and work data"},{"u":"start/#next","p":"Start here","h":"For everyone","t":"","x":"For everyone The 12 moves that make answers better. Power features most people never find. The 5-day challenge: ten minutes a day. Safety and privacy before you paste anything sensitive. When it breaks if something is missing."},{"u":"what-is-copilot/","p":"Your licence: what you've got","h":"","t":"","x":"Guide Your licence: what you've got What the label under your name means, and exactly what it lets Copilot do for you."},{"u":"what-is-copilot/","p":"Your licence: what you've got","h":"The short version","t":"","x":"The short version This site covers two tiers: Chat (free with eligible plans) and Premium (the paid add-on). The main difference is whether Copilot can reach your work data on its own. Microsoft recently renamed things. Old and new names are both in use. Use the lookup table to check any feature against your licence."},{"u":"what-is-copilot/#names","p":"Your licence: what you've got","h":"The names changed","t":"","x":"The names changed Microsoft's own documentation now says that Microsoft 365 Copilot is called Microsoft Copilot, and Microsoft 365 Copilot Chat is called Microsoft Copilot Chat. Pages and menus still use the old names during the transition. The web address is now copilot.cloud.microsoft. In practice: if one article says \"Microsoft 365 Copilot\" and another says \"Microsoft Copilot\" they are usually describing the same product. Do not mix it up with the free consumer Copilot or with GitHub Copilot, which is for programmers. Those are separate. Under your name you will see one of three labels. On this site: Copilot Chat (Basic) and M365 Copilot (Basic) are the track. M365 Copilot (Premium) is the track. Usage-based extras such as Copilot Cowork and Copilot Code need the Premium licence and are billed by consumption. They are not covered yet; a later section will cover them."},{"u":"what-is-copilot/#chat","p":"Your licence: what you've got","h":"With Copilot Chat","t":"chat","x":"With Copilot Chat What you get: a secure AI chat included with eligible Microsoft 365 plans when you sign in with a work or school account. It works from the web, and also in Outlook and Teams. Two Chat labels, and Word/Excel/PowerPoint. Microsoft's overview says people with the Copilot Chat (Basic) label \"don't have access\" to Copilot in Word, Excel, PowerPoint and OneNote, while people with M365 Copilot (Basic) have standard access to it, subject to service capacity. Microsoft's Learn requirements page adds that chat in Word, Excel and PowerPoint varies with licence and tenant configuration. What it can see: Chat cannot use Microsoft Graph, the layer that holds your emails, files, meetings and calendar. It can use your content only if you hand it over: paste it, upload a file, or open it next to Copilot in Outlook or Teams. Copilot only shows you data you already have at least view permission for. The 30-second test Open Word and look for the Copilot button. If it is there, you have Copilot in the Office apps. If it is not, you have the web chat, Outlook and Teams, and every playbook on this site still has a route for you. Microsoft's pages don't explain what decides which Basic label a person gets, and reports say the in-app pane ended for unlicensed users in April 2026, so your own apps are the reliable answer. Button missing when you expected it? See Fix it."},{"u":"what-is-copilot/#premium","p":"Your licence: what you've got","h":"With M365 Copilot (Premium)","t":"premium","x":"With M365 Copilot (Premium) What you get: the paid add-on. Everything in Chat, plus priority access, Copilot Search, agents that use work data, and management tools for administrators. What it can see: it pulls from Graph and Work IQ automatically. You can name a file to focus on, but you do not have to upload it. An administrator can switch Work IQ off, in which case answers are not enriched with your work context. It still only shows you data you already have at least view permission to open. To check whether Work IQ is working for you, ask Copilot about a file you know you can open; if it can't find it, see Troubleshooting."},{"u":"what-is-copilot/#lookup","p":"Your licence: what you've got","h":"Can my Copilot do this?","t":"","x":"Can my Copilot do this? Type a word to filter. \"Chat\" covers both Basic labels. The less obvious features are explained in Power features. Filter Feature Chat with Copilot under your work account's data protection Yes Yes Answers from the web Yes, unless your organisation turns web search off Yes, unless your organisation turns web search off Upload or paste a file into the chat Yes Yes Find your emails, files, meetings and chats for you No. You hand it the content Yes Copilot in Outlook: inbox, calendar, drafting, scheduling Yes Yes, plus your chats and documents Copilot Chat in Teams and Outlook that sees the item you have open Yes Yes Copilot inside Word, Excel, PowerPoint and OneNote Depends on your label: no on Copilot Chat (Basic), standard access on M365 Copilot (Basic) Yes, with priority access Teams meeting recap and questions Needs Teams Premium or a Copilot licence, and the organiser's setting Yes, within the organiser's setting Choose Auto, Quick response or Think deeper Yes Yes Summarise the web page or PDF open in Edge Yes Yes Copilot Pages: turn an answer into an editable, shareable page Yes Yes Copilot Notebooks Yes, streamlined: up to 50 sources, no meetings or web links Yes, in full: up to 300 sources Memory and custom instructions Yes, unless your organisation turns personalisation off Yes, unless your organisation turns personalisation off Scheduled prompts No Yes, up to 10 Copilot Search across your apps No Yes Generate images in chat If your organisation allows it If your organisation allows it Researcher and Analyst agents No Yes, if your organisation has them switched on Coaching agents: Prompt, Writing, Idea, Learning, Career Yes, if your organisation has enabled them Yes, if your organisation has enabled them Build your own agent from instructions and websites Probably. Look for New agent in the Copilot app Yes Build an agent that reads SharePoint files Only if your organisation pays as it goes Yes Build an agent that uses your email, Teams and people data No Yes Create an agent from a SharePoint library Only with pay-as-you-go, plus edit rights on the site Yes, with edit rights on the site Copilot Cowork and Copilot Code No Extra, billed by usage. Not covered here yet \"Yes\" means Microsoft's documentation says the licence includes it. Your organisation can still switch individual features off, so the final test is what appears in your own apps."},{"u":"what-is-copilot/#check","p":"Your licence: what you've got","h":"Check your own setup","t":"","x":"Check your own setup Which label do I see under my name at copilot.cloud.microsoft ? Is my licence assigned? Microsoft publishes a licence details page at aka.ms/CopilotLicenseDetails. Do I see the Work label and green shield? Is there a View info from your organization link in the app? That is where your organisation puts its own guidance. Web search, agents and models are organisation settings. You cannot change them, but you can see what is switched on by what appears in your own menus."},{"u":"moves/","p":"The 12 moves","h":"","t":"","x":"Technique The 12 moves Most weak answers come from weak asks. These twelve habits work on every licence, and each one takes a sentence."},{"u":"moves/","p":"The 12 moves","h":"The short version","t":"","x":"The short version Say the goal, who it is for, what to use and what you want back. Microsoft's own recipe has those four parts. Treat the first answer as a draft and reply to it. Make Copilot question you, criticise itself and admit what it doesn't know."},{"u":"moves/#ask","p":"The 12 moves","h":"Asking well","t":"","x":"Asking well 1. Lead with the goal Start with the task and its purpose. The goal is the only part Microsoft calls required. Move 1 Write a short update for [audience] so they can decide whether to [decision]. 2. Say who it is for Audience changes vocabulary, length and what gets explained. Move 2 The reader is [who]. They know [what they already know] and care most about [what they care about]. 3. Hand it the source Point at the material and fence it in. \"Using only\" stops it filling gaps from elsewhere. Move 3 Using only [the text below / the attached file], and nothing else, answer this: [question] 4. Describe the output Format, length and tone. If you want a table, say which columns. Move 4 Give me a table with columns [column names]. No more than [8] rows. Plain words, no jargon. 5. Show an example One sample of what good looks like beats a paragraph describing it. Move 5 Here is an example of the style I want: [Paste an example here] Write the new one in the same style and length."},{"u":"moves/#steer","p":"The 12 moves","h":"Steering the answer","t":"","x":"Steering the answer 6. Reply, don't restart Microsoft expects you to refine. A follow-up keeps everything it already knows. Move 6 Shorter, less formal, and put the recommendation first. 7. Ask for options Three versions let you choose instead of accept. Move 7 Give me three versions: one cautious, one direct, one persuasive. Say which you would send and why. 8. Make it interview you When you can't describe what you need, answer questions instead. Move 8 I want help with [task]. Ask me up to five questions, one at a time. When you have enough, write the best prompt for the job and then answer it. 9. Work in steps Outline, then draft, then polish. You catch a wrong turn early. Move 9 Step 1 only: give me an outline. Wait for my OK before you write anything else."},{"u":"moves/#trust","p":"The 12 moves","h":"Trusting the answer","t":"","x":"Trusting the answer 10. Forbid guessing Responses are not guaranteed to be factual. Give it permission to say it doesn't know. Move 10 If the answer is not in the source, say \"not stated\". Do not guess or fill gaps. 11. Make it criticise its own work It is better at finding faults when asked than at avoiding them unasked. Move 11 Now act as a sceptical reviewer. List the three weakest points in what you just wrote, then fix them. 12. Ask where it came from Then open the source. For anything that matters, run the prompt twice: answers vary. Move 12 For each claim, show where it comes from: quote the line or give the link. Mark anything you could not verify."},{"u":"moves/#keep","p":"The 12 moves","h":"Keep what works","t":"","x":"Keep what works When a prompt works, save it. Hover over it in Copilot and look for the bookmark icon, Save prompt. Or keep a note or a document called \"My prompts\" and paste the good ones in, with the blanks marked in [square brackets]. In a month you will have your own library that fits your job better than any website's. Tired of repeating your preferences? Put them in custom instructions once. Next: put the moves to work in a playbook, or take the 5-day challenge."},{"u":"extras/","p":"Power features","h":"","t":"","x":"Beyond the chat box Power features The features most people never find. Several of them work on the free Copilot Chat licence."},{"u":"extras/","p":"Power features","h":"The short version","t":"","x":"The short version Custom instructions and memory: tell Copilot once how you like things. Pages: turn a good answer into a document you can edit and share. Notebooks: gather the files for one piece of work and question them together. Edge: summarise the web page or PDF in front of you. Saved prompts: stop retyping. can also run prompts on a schedule. Microsoft is rolling out a new Copilot home screen, so a menu may sit in a slightly different place from the steps below. Your organisation can also switch any of these off."},{"u":"extras/#instructions","p":"Power features","h":"Custom instructions: set it once","t":"","x":"Custom instructions: set it once Custom instructions tell Copilot your preferences, and it applies them in future chats. In Copilot Chat, select … Settings and more at the top right, then Chat settings. Choose Personalization, find Custom instructions and select Edit instructions. Write your preferences and select Save instructions. Custom instructions to start from I work as [role] in [team or industry]. Use UK spelling and plain words. Lead with the answer, then the reasoning. Use short bullets for lists. If you are not sure, say so. Never guess a number, a name or a date. If the toggle is off and won't turn on, your organisation has switched personalisation off."},{"u":"extras/#memory","p":"Power features","h":"Memory: what Copilot keeps about you","t":"","x":"Memory: what Copilot keeps about you With Copilot Memory on, Copilot saves key details from your chats, such as your style and recurring tasks. Microsoft says memory is available with and without a Copilot licence. Ask \"What do you know about me?\" to see it. Delete memories in Chat settings. For a chat you don't want remembered, select Start a new temporary chat at the top right. Your organisation's retention rules can still keep a copy. Memories are stored in your mailbox and administrators can search them, like the rest of your Copilot history. See Safety."},{"u":"extras/#pages","p":"Power features","h":"Pages: keep and share a good answer","t":"","x":"Pages: keep and share a good answer A Copilot Page turns a chat answer into content you can edit, refine with Copilot and work on with colleagues. Work accounts with SharePoint or OneDrive storage have it, including people without a Copilot licence. Under a Copilot answer, select the … menu, then Edit in Pages. Choose a new or existing page. The page opens beside the chat. Rename it, edit it, and keep asking Copilot to improve it. Share it with colleagues. Earlier pages are in the Copilot Library. Use it whenever an answer is worth keeping: a draft, a plan, meeting notes, a comparison table."},{"u":"extras/#edge","p":"Power features","h":"Edge: summarise what is in front of you","t":"","x":"Edge: summarise what is in front of you Select the Copilot icon at the top right of Edge, signed in with your work account. Copilot Chat can summarise website content and some document types shown in Edge. Prompt · in Edge Summarise this page for [who]. Give me the five points that matter, anything that looks out of date, and what I should read in full."},{"u":"extras/#saved","p":"Power features","h":"Saved prompts: stop retyping","t":"","x":"Saved prompts: stop retyping After a prompt has run, hover over it and select the bookmark icon, Save prompt. It appears under Your saved prompts in the Copilot Prompt Lab, visible only to you. Microsoft's page doesn't state a licence for this, so look for the bookmark. If it isn't there, keep a \"My prompts\" note instead."},{"u":"extras/#images","p":"Power features","h":"Images","t":"","x":"Images Where your organisation allows image generation, ask for an image in Copilot Chat, or use the Visual Creator agent."},{"u":"extras/#chat","p":"Power features","h":"Notebooks on Copilot Chat","t":"chat","x":"Notebooks on Copilot Chat A notebook gathers the files, pages and notes for one piece of work, so Copilot answers from all of them. Eligible Copilot Chat users get a streamlined version. Go to m365.cloud.microsoft and select Notebooks in the left menu, or find it in the app launcher. Select New notebook, name it, and add references: upload, drag and drop, or pick from OneDrive. Select Create, then ask questions across everything in it. You get: up to 50 sources per notebook, plus mind maps, infographics, study guides, reports, flashcards and quizzes. You can add: PDFs, text and media files, Word, Excel and PowerPoint files, OneNote and Loop pages, and email threads. Not on Chat: web links, Teams chats and meetings as sources, suggested references, Audio Overviews, and Researcher inside a notebook. If Notebooks is missing or shows a licence message, it is not switched on for you. Uploading the files to an ordinary chat is the fallback. Scheduled prompts need a Copilot licence, so they are not on this track."},{"u":"extras/#premium","p":"Power features","h":"Notebooks, scheduling and search on Premium","t":"premium","x":"Notebooks, scheduling and search on Premium Notebooks, in full Create one the same way: m365.cloud.microsoft, Notebooks, New notebook, add references, Create. With a licence you get: Up to 300 sources per notebook, with suggested references. Web links, Teams chats, meetings, and SharePoint and OneDrive content as sources. Researcher inside the notebook, up to 25 a month. Audio Overviews, up to 20 a day, and Live Capture. A Word, Excel or PowerPoint document created from the notebook in one go. Scheduled prompts Have a prompt run by itself, for example a Monday summary of what needs your attention. In Copilot Chat, hover over a prompt you have sent and select Schedule this prompt. Choose when and how often, and whether to get an email when it is ready. Select Save. Manage them under … Settings and more, then Scheduled prompts. You can have up to 10. Prompt · worth scheduling Summarise what needs my attention this week from my emails, meetings and chats. List deadlines, things waiting on me, and meetings I should prepare for. Name the source of each. Copilot Search Copilot Search is one search across your Microsoft 365 apps and connected sources. Microsoft lists it with the Premium licence."},{"u":"copilot-way/","p":"The Copilot way","h":"","t":"","x":"Translation guide The Copilot way Friends and articles talk about what they do with other AI tools. Your workplace gives you Copilot. Here is the nearest route for each thing, on your licence."},{"u":"copilot-way/","p":"The Copilot way","h":"The short version","t":"","x":"The short version Most everyday jobs have a Copilot route on both licences. Chat users get there by handing Copilot the material. Premium users can let it find the material. The less obvious ones are explained in Power features."},{"u":"copilot-way/#chat","p":"The Copilot way","h":"On Copilot Chat","t":"chat","x":"On Copilot Chat You've heard of… The Copilot way Chatting with an AI about anything Copilot Chat at copilot.cloud.microsoft, signed in with your work account. Uploading a document and asking about it Upload or select the file with your prompt. See Summarise a long document. A slower \"thinking\" mode for hard questions Choose Think deeper in the model selector. Web research with sources Ask for cited findings in ordinary chat. See Research a topic. The Researcher agent needs a Copilot licence. A custom assistant set up for one job An agent. Look for New agent in the Copilot app. On Chat it can use instructions and websites. See Make your own agent. Help analysing data Paste a small table or upload the file and ask for trends and formulas. See Make sense of a spreadsheet. Making images The Visual Creator agent, where your organisation has it. An AI that reads your email Copilot in Outlook works with your inbox and calendar on this licence. See Clear my inbox. A canvas or document that grows out of the chat Copilot Pages: Edit in Pages under any answer. See Power features. A project space that holds your files Copilot Notebooks, with up to 50 sources on this licence. See Power features. An AI that remembers your preferences Memory and custom instructions. See Power features. Summarising the web page you are on The Copilot icon in Edge. A writing or prompt coach Writing Coach and Prompt Coach agents, via All agents."},{"u":"copilot-way/#premium","p":"The Copilot way","h":"On M365 Copilot (Premium)","t":"premium","x":"On M365 Copilot (Premium) You've heard of… The Copilot way Chatting with an AI about anything Copilot Chat, which can also draw on your emails, files, meetings and chats. Uploading a document and asking about it Name the file. No upload needed. See Summarise a long document. A slower \"thinking\" mode for hard questions Choose Think deeper in the model selector. \"Deep research\" reports The Researcher agent: slow on purpose, with a cited report. See Researcher. A custom assistant set up for one job An agent built in Agent Builder, which can use SharePoint, your email and Teams as knowledge. See Make your own agent. Help analysing data Copilot in Excel, or the Analyst agent. See Make sense of a spreadsheet. Making images The Visual Creator agent. An AI that writes inside your documents Copilot in Word, Excel and PowerPoint. See the app guides. A canvas or document that grows out of the chat Copilot Pages: Edit in Pages under any answer. See Power features. A project space that holds your files Copilot Notebooks, with up to 300 sources, meetings and chats. See Power features. An AI that remembers your preferences Memory and custom instructions. See Power features. Tasks that run on a schedule Scheduled prompts, up to 10. See Power features. Meeting notes written for you Copilot in Teams meetings, where the organiser allows it. See Follow up a meeting."},{"u":"copilot-way/#unchecked","p":"The Copilot way","h":"Not checked yet","t":"","x":"Not checked yet Voice conversations and file-size limits for uploads are not covered here yet. The quickest test is your own Copilot app."},{"u":"challenge/","p":"The 5-day challenge","h":"","t":"","x":"Practice The 5-day challenge Ten minutes a day, on real work. By Friday you will have five habits and your own short list of prompts that work. Ticks are saved in this browser only. Nobody else sees them. With JavaScript off, the boxes still work but are not remembered."},{"u":"challenge/","p":"The 5-day challenge","h":"Day 1 · Know what you've got","t":"","x":"Day 1 · Know what you've got Check your label and set My licence at the top of this page. Start here shows how. Send the first prompt from Start here about something real. Read the self-check in Safety."},{"u":"challenge/","p":"The 5-day challenge","h":"Day 2 · Email","t":"","x":"Day 2 · Email Run the Clear my inbox playbook. Send one reply that started as a Copilot draft and that you edited."},{"u":"challenge/","p":"The 5-day challenge","h":"Day 3 · Documents","t":"","x":"Day 3 · Documents Run Summarise a long document on something you have been putting off reading. Use move 10 and move 12 from The 12 moves, then open one source to check it."},{"u":"challenge/","p":"The 5-day challenge","h":"Day 4 · Meetings","t":"","x":"Day 4 · Meetings Run Prepare for a meeting before your next one. Run Follow up a meeting afterwards and send the notes."},{"u":"challenge/","p":"The 5-day challenge","h":"Day 5 · Make it yours","t":"","x":"Day 5 · Make it yours Pick one more playbook that fits your job and run it. Start a \"My prompts\" note and save the three prompts that worked best this week. Set your custom instructions so every answer starts closer to what you want. Open Agents in the Copilot app and try one. The agents overview says what to expect on your licence."},{"u":"playbooks/","p":"Playbooks","h":"","t":"","x":"Playbooks What do you want to get done? Twelve everyday jobs. Each one gives the best route on your licence, a prompt you can fill in and copy, follow-ups to push the answer further, and what to check before you use it. Clear my inbox Triage unread mail, draft replies, flag what can wait. Write a first draft From rough notes to a structured draft you can edit. Shorten or sharpen Make existing text shorter, clearer or better pitched. Summarise a long document Get the point, the risks and the gaps from a long file. Prepare for a meeting Agenda, questions and likely objections in five minutes. Follow up a meeting Decisions, actions and owners, sent while it is fresh. Make sense of a spreadsheet Trends, outliers and formulas without breaking the file. Build a deck A structured first deck from a prompt or a document. Research a topic A sourced answer you can check, not a confident guess. Plan a piece of work Steps, risks and a first week, pressure-tested. Learn something fast A personal tutor for a topic, a tool or a piece of jargon. Catch up after time away Back from leave: what happened, what is waiting on you. Set My licence at the top of the page and each playbook shows only your route. Not sure which you have? Start here."},{"u":"inbox/","p":"Clear my inbox","h":"","t":"","x":"Playbook Clear my inbox Let Copilot sort the pile, then answer the three emails that matter."},{"u":"inbox/#chat","p":"Clear my inbox","h":"With Copilot Chat","t":"chat","x":"With Copilot Chat Open Copilot in Outlook Select the Copilot button near the top of Outlook. On the Chat licence it can still work with your inbox and calendar. Triage first Send the prompt below. Don't reply to anything yet. Draft one reply at a time Open the email, then ask for a draft. Read it, fix it, send it yourself. Prompt · Chat Summarise my unread emails from [this week] into three groups: needs a reply from me, for information only, and can wait. For each one give the sender and a one-line reason. Then name the three I should answer first, and why."},{"u":"inbox/#premium","p":"Clear my inbox","h":"With M365 Copilot (Premium)","t":"premium","x":"With M365 Copilot (Premium) Open Copilot in Outlook Same button. With your licence it can also draw on your Teams chats and documents. Triage with context Send the prompt below. It can check what was said elsewhere before you reply. Draft, check, send Ask for a draft of each reply. Read it, fix it, send it yourself. Prompt · Premium Summarise my unread emails from [this week] into three groups: needs a reply from me, for information only, and can wait. For the three most urgent, check related files and Teams chats and say what I need to know before I reply. Name the source of each point."},{"u":"inbox/#further","p":"Clear my inbox","h":"Push it further","t":"","x":"Push it further The first answer is a draft. Send one of these next: Follow-up Draft a reply to the first one. I want to [accept / decline / ask for more time]. Under 100 words, one clear next step. Follow-up Which of these are waiting on someone else, and since when? Follow-up Flag the ones that can wait until Friday."},{"u":"inbox/#check","p":"Clear my inbox","h":"Before you use it","t":"","x":"Before you use it Copilot drafts. You send. Read every reply before it goes. Check names, dates and anything you are promising. A summary can miss the one line that matters. Open anything important. Results vary by licence and by your files. If a prompt misses, reply to Copilot with what to change."},{"u":"first-draft/","p":"Write a first draft","h":"","t":"","x":"Playbook Write a first draft Never start from a blank page. Start from an outline you agreed with Copilot."},{"u":"first-draft/#chat","p":"Write a first draft","h":"With Copilot Chat","t":"chat","x":"With Copilot Chat Open Copilot Chat Go to copilot.cloud.microsoft, or the Copilot icon in the Edge sidebar. Agree the outline first Paste your notes and send the prompt below. Fix the outline before any writing happens. Draft section by section Say \"write section 1\". Shorter requests give better text. Keep it in a Page Under a good answer choose Edit in Pages to keep editing and share it, or paste it into Word. Have Copilot in Word? If your label is M365 Copilot (Basic), look for the Copilot button in Word and work there. Prompt · Chat I need to write [document type] for [audience] about [topic]. Here are my notes: [Paste your notes here] First give me an outline of five headings with one line under each. Wait for my OK before you write anything."},{"u":"first-draft/#premium","p":"Write a first draft","h":"With M365 Copilot (Premium)","t":"premium","x":"With M365 Copilot (Premium) Open Copilot in Word Blank document, then the Copilot icon at the bottom right. Point it at your sources Type / to pick a file. It can also use your recent emails. Generate, then steer Keep, regenerate or refine with a follow-up. Select any paragraph to rewrite just that part. Prompt · Premium Draft [document type] for [audience] about [topic], using /[file name] and my recent emails with [person]. Start with a three-line summary. Use short headings. Keep it to [one page]. Mark anything you were unsure of with the word CHECK."},{"u":"first-draft/#further","p":"Write a first draft","h":"Push it further","t":"","x":"Push it further The first answer is a draft. Send one of these next: Follow-up Rewrite the opening three ways: formal, friendly and blunt. Follow-up What would a sceptical reader challenge? List the three weakest points. Follow-up Cut this by a third without losing any number or date."},{"u":"first-draft/#check","p":"Write a first draft","h":"Before you use it","t":"","x":"Before you use it Facts, figures, names and policy statements: verify each one. Does it sound like you? Rewrite the first and last paragraph yourself. Check the sensitivity label on the finished document. Results vary by licence and by your files. If a prompt misses, reply to Copilot with what to change."},{"u":"rewrite/","p":"Shorten or sharpen","h":"","t":"","x":"Playbook Shorten or sharpen You have the words. Copilot is quick at cutting them down and changing the tone."},{"u":"rewrite/#chat","p":"Shorten or sharpen","h":"With Copilot Chat","t":"chat","x":"With Copilot Chat Paste the text into Copilot Chat Anything you are allowed to share. See what not to paste. Say who it is for Audience and length matter more than \"make it better\". Ask what it removed So nothing important disappears quietly. Prompt · Chat Rewrite the text below for [audience] in about half the length. Keep every number, date and name. Use plain words and short sentences. After the rewrite, list anything you removed that they might still ask about. [Paste the text here]"},{"u":"rewrite/#premium","p":"Shorten or sharpen","h":"With M365 Copilot (Premium)","t":"premium","x":"With M365 Copilot (Premium) Select the text in Word Then ask Copilot to rewrite it, change the tone or shorten it. Or restructure the whole document Use Edit with Copilot for bigger changes. Compare before you accept Read the old and new versions side by side. Prompt · Premium Rewrite the selected text for [audience] in about half the length. Keep every number, date and name. Use plain words and short sentences. Then list anything you removed that they might still ask about."},{"u":"rewrite/#further","p":"Shorten or sharpen","h":"Push it further","t":"","x":"Push it further The first answer is a draft. Send one of these next: Follow-up Give me three versions: neutral, warmer, firmer. Follow-up Turn this into five bullet points for a slide. Follow-up What is unclear or ambiguous in this text? Quote the phrase."},{"u":"rewrite/#check","p":"Shorten or sharpen","h":"Before you use it","t":"","x":"Before you use it Numbers and dates survived the cut. The meaning has not shifted. Shorter text can sound more certain than you are. Nothing confidential went into the wrong Copilot. Look for Work and the green shield. Results vary by licence and by your files. If a prompt misses, reply to Copilot with what to change."},{"u":"summarise/","p":"Summarise a long document","h":"","t":"","x":"Playbook Summarise a long document Ask for a summary built for a decision, and make Copilot say what the document leaves out."},{"u":"summarise/#chat","p":"Summarise a long document","h":"With Copilot Chat","t":"chat","x":"With Copilot Chat Give it the document Upload the file or select it when you write the prompt. Chat does not go and find it for you. Ask for a purpose-built summary Say who is reading and what they must decide. Reading a web page or PDF? Open it in Edge and select the Copilot icon. Copilot Chat can summarise what is on screen. Prompt · Chat Using only the attached document, summarise it for [who], who must decide [what]. Give me: the three most important points, the risks or open questions, and what is missing from the document. If something is not in the document, say so. Do not guess."},{"u":"summarise/#premium","p":"Summarise a long document","h":"With M365 Copilot (Premium)","t":"premium","x":"With M365 Copilot (Premium) Name the file No upload needed. Copilot can open anything you can. Ask for a purpose-built summary Say who is reading and what they must decide. Compare documents Name two files and ask what changed between them. Prompt · Premium Using only /[file name], summarise it for [who], who must decide [what]. Give me: the three most important points, the risks or open questions, and what is missing from the document. If something is not in the document, say so. Do not guess."},{"u":"summarise/#further","p":"Summarise a long document","h":"Push it further","t":"","x":"Push it further The first answer is a draft. Send one of these next: Follow-up List every date, number and commitment in a table, with the page or section. Follow-up What are the three weakest arguments in this document? Follow-up Write five questions I should ask the author."},{"u":"summarise/#check","p":"Summarise a long document","h":"Before you use it","t":"","x":"Before you use it Open the document at two or three of the points and confirm they are there. Summaries flatten caveats. Read the section behind anything you will act on. Ask \"where does that come from?\" for any surprising claim. Results vary by licence and by your files. If a prompt misses, reply to Copilot with what to change."},{"u":"meeting-prep/","p":"Prepare for a meeting","h":"","t":"","x":"Playbook Prepare for a meeting Walk in knowing your goal, your questions and what the other side will push back on."},{"u":"meeting-prep/#chat","p":"Prepare for a meeting","h":"With Copilot Chat","t":"chat","x":"With Copilot Chat Open Copilot in Outlook It can answer questions about your calendar and meetings on the Chat licence. Give it the background Paste the invite text, the last email, or your own notes. Ask for a one-page brief Use the prompt below. Prompt · Chat I have a [30]-minute meeting with [who] about [topic]. My goal is [outcome]. Background: [Paste any background here] Give me a timed agenda, three questions I should ask, and two objections I should be ready for. Keep it to one page."},{"u":"meeting-prep/#premium","p":"Prepare for a meeting","h":"With M365 Copilot (Premium)","t":"premium","x":"With M365 Copilot (Premium) Ask from anywhere Copilot Chat, Outlook or Teams. It can read the emails, chats and files behind the meeting. Name the person or the meeting It finds the history for you. Ask what you owe them The most useful line in the prompt below. Prompt · Premium Prepare me for my meeting with [person] on [day]. Use our recent emails, chats and shared files. Give me: a one-line goal, where we left things, three questions to ask, two objections to expect, and anything I promised and have not done. Name the source of each point."},{"u":"meeting-prep/#further","p":"Prepare for a meeting","h":"Push it further","t":"","x":"Push it further The first answer is a draft. Send one of these next: Follow-up Draft a two-line message I can send beforehand to set the agenda. Follow-up Play the other side. What will they want from this meeting? Follow-up What decision should we leave the room with?"},{"u":"meeting-prep/#check","p":"Prepare for a meeting","h":"Before you use it","t":"","x":"Before you use it Check \"where we left things\" against your own memory. Anything it says you promised: open the source. The agenda is a draft. Cut it to fit the time. Results vary by licence and by your files. If a prompt misses, reply to Copilot with what to change."},{"u":"meeting-follow-up/","p":"Follow up a meeting","h":"","t":"","x":"Playbook Follow up a meeting Notes out within the hour, with owners and dates, and nothing invented."},{"u":"meeting-follow-up/#chat","p":"Follow up a meeting","h":"With Copilot Chat","t":"chat","x":"With Copilot Chat Get the words Copy your notes, or the transcript from the meeting chat if one was made. Paste them into Copilot Chat In Teams, Copilot Chat can also see the item you have open. Ask for actions as a table Tell it to write \"not stated\" instead of guessing an owner. Prompt · Chat Below are the notes from our meeting. Write follow-up notes for the people who attended. Include decisions, action items as a table (action, owner, due date), and open questions. Use only what is in the notes. Write \"not stated\" if an owner or date is missing. [Paste the notes here]"},{"u":"meeting-follow-up/#premium","p":"Follow up a meeting","h":"With M365 Copilot (Premium)","t":"premium","x":"With M365 Copilot (Premium) Open the meeting recap From the meeting chat or the Recap tab in Teams. Ask Copilot there It works from the transcript, so the organiser must have allowed Copilot during and after the meeting. Send the notes yourself Long answers can open in Word. Prompt · Premium Write follow-up notes for the people who attended this meeting. Include decisions, action items as a table (action, owner, due date), and open questions. Use only what was said. Write \"not stated\" if an owner or date is missing, and quote the line each action came from."},{"u":"meeting-follow-up/#further","p":"Follow up a meeting","h":"Push it further","t":"","x":"Push it further The first answer is a draft. Send one of these next: Follow-up Draft the email that sends these notes. Three lines, then the table. Follow-up What was discussed but not decided? Follow-up Which actions are mine?"},{"u":"meeting-follow-up/#check","p":"Follow up a meeting","h":"Before you use it","t":"","x":"Before you use it Summaries can misattribute or soften what someone said. Check anything about a person. Confirm owners and dates with the people named. No transcript, no after-meeting answers. See the organiser's setting. Results vary by licence and by your files. If a prompt misses, reply to Copilot with what to change."},{"u":"spreadsheet/","p":"Make sense of a spreadsheet","h":"","t":"","x":"Playbook Make sense of a spreadsheet Ask questions of the data first. Change the workbook second, on a copy."},{"u":"spreadsheet/#chat","p":"Make sense of a spreadsheet","h":"With Copilot Chat","t":"chat","x":"With Copilot Chat Give it the data Upload the file to Copilot Chat or paste a small table. Check what you may share first. Ask what stands out Chat explains and writes formulas. It does not change your workbook. Copy the formula across Paste it into Excel and test it on a few rows. Have Copilot in Excel? If the button is there, use Chat only mode to explore safely. Prompt · Chat Here is a table of data: [Paste the data here] Tell me three things that stand out and one thing that looks like an error or an outlier. Explain how you know. Then write a formula for column [D] that [describes what it should calculate], and explain it in one sentence."},{"u":"spreadsheet/#premium","p":"Make sense of a spreadsheet","h":"With M365 Copilot (Premium)","t":"premium","x":"With M365 Copilot (Premium) Open Copilot in Excel Pick the mode before you ask: Chat only to explore, Plan to see changes first, Allow editing on a copy. Explore, then build Ask what stands out. Then ask for the summary table or chart. Bigger question? Try the Analyst agent. Prompt · Premium Look at the data on this sheet. Tell me three things that stand out and one thing that looks like an error or an outlier. Then show me your plan for a summary table of [measure] by [category] and [month], with a chart of the trend. Use formulas, not pasted values. Do not change anything until I say so."},{"u":"spreadsheet/#further","p":"Make sense of a spreadsheet","h":"Push it further","t":"","x":"Push it further The first answer is a draft. Send one of these next: Follow-up Explain this formula step by step: [Paste the formula here] Follow-up What happens to the result if a cell in column [B] is empty? Follow-up Which rows did you exclude, and why?"},{"u":"spreadsheet/#check","p":"Make sense of a spreadsheet","h":"Before you use it","t":"","x":"Before you use it Click into the formulas. Do they point at the right ranges? Recalculate one total by hand. After a sort or filter, count the rows. Results vary by licence and by your files. If a prompt misses, reply to Copilot with what to change."},{"u":"deck/","p":"Build a deck","h":"","t":"","x":"Playbook Build a deck Get the story right in outline form. Slides come second."},{"u":"deck/#chat","p":"Build a deck","h":"With Copilot Chat","t":"chat","x":"With Copilot Chat Outline in Copilot Chat Paste your notes and ask for a slide-by-slide outline. Tighten the story Ask it to cut slides and sharpen titles until each title makes one point. Build the slides yourself Paste titles and bullets into your organisation's template. Have Copilot in PowerPoint? If the Copilot button is there, follow the Premium route. Prompt · Chat Draft an outline for an [8]-slide presentation for [audience] about [topic]. Purpose: help them decide [decision]. For each slide give a title that makes one point, three short bullets, and one suggested visual. Use only the notes below. [Paste your notes here]"},{"u":"deck/#premium","p":"Build a deck","h":"With M365 Copilot (Premium)","t":"premium","x":"With M365 Copilot (Premium) Start from your template Open a new presentation from your organisation's template. Open Copilot, then Add Content Choose Agent Mode. It may ask about audience and style, then shows an outline first. Or start from a Word document It pulls the structure and images across. Smaller files work best. Prompt · Premium Create an [8]-slide presentation for [audience] about [topic]. Purpose: help them decide [decision]. Structure: the problem, three options with pros and cons, a recommendation, next steps. One idea per slide. Keep slide text short. Show me the outline before you build it."},{"u":"deck/#further","p":"Build a deck","h":"Push it further","t":"","x":"Push it further The first answer is a draft. Send one of these next: Follow-up Rewrite every slide title so it states the conclusion. Follow-up Write speaker notes for slide [3], about 60 seconds long. Follow-up What questions will this audience ask? Add a backup slide for the hardest one."},{"u":"deck/#check","p":"Build a deck","h":"Before you use it","t":"","x":"Before you use it Never trust a generated statistic. Trace each number. Check images for brand and audience fit. Cut. First drafts have too much text. Results vary by licence and by your files. If a prompt misses, reply to Copilot with what to change."},{"u":"research/","p":"Research a topic","h":"","t":"","x":"Playbook Research a topic Make Copilot show its sources and its doubts."},{"u":"research/#chat","p":"Research a topic","h":"With Copilot Chat","t":"chat","x":"With Copilot Chat Use Copilot Chat on the web It searches the web when that helps. Pick the slower setting Choose Think deeper in the model selector for a more thorough answer. Demand sources and disagreements Use the prompt below, then open the links. Collecting documents? Put them in a notebook, up to 50 sources, and question them together. Prompt · Chat Research [topic] using public web sources, for a decision on [decision]. Give me the key findings, where sources disagree, the risks, and a recommendation. Cite every claim with its link. Mark anything you could not verify."},{"u":"research/#premium","p":"Research a topic","h":"With M365 Copilot (Premium)","t":"premium","x":"With M365 Copilot (Premium) Open the Researcher agent In the Agents pane. It is slow on purpose and returns a cited report. Set the scope Web, your work sources, or both. Answer its questions It may ask before it starts. That steers the report. Prompt · Premium Research [topic] for a decision on [decision]. Scope: [the web only / our internal documents and emails only / both]. I need the key findings, where sources disagree, the risks, and a recommendation. Cite every claim. Mark anything you could not verify."},{"u":"research/#further","p":"Research a topic","h":"Push it further","t":"","x":"Push it further The first answer is a draft. Send one of these next: Follow-up What is the strongest argument against your recommendation? Follow-up Which of these sources is least reliable, and why? Follow-up Summarise this in five lines for someone senior."},{"u":"research/#check","p":"Research a topic","h":"Before you use it","t":"","x":"Before you use it Open the citations behind anything you will repeat. A link is not proof. Check the page says what Copilot says it says. Run it twice. Answers vary. Results vary by licence and by your files. If a prompt misses, reply to Copilot with what to change."},{"u":"plan/","p":"Plan a piece of work","h":"","t":"","x":"Playbook Plan a piece of work Have Copilot interview you, then attack the plan it helps you write."},{"u":"plan/#chat","p":"Plan a piece of work","h":"With Copilot Chat","t":"chat","x":"With Copilot Chat Let it ask the questions Start with the prompt below. Answering five questions beats writing a long brief. Get the plan Ask for steps, owners, risks and the first week. Attack it Ask for the three weakest assumptions. Keep it alive Choose Edit in Pages under the plan so you and your colleagues can keep updating it. Prompt · Chat I need to plan [piece of work]. The deadline is [date] and the people involved are [who]. Ask me up to five questions, one at a time, to understand it. Then write a plan: steps in order, who does each, the main risks, and what I should do in the first week."},{"u":"plan/#premium","p":"Plan a piece of work","h":"With M365 Copilot (Premium)","t":"premium","x":"With M365 Copilot (Premium) Ground it in what exists Name the project. Copilot can read the emails, files and meetings about it. Get the plan Ask for steps, owners, risks and the first week. Attack it Ask for the three weakest assumptions. Prompt · Premium I need to plan [piece of work]. Use my emails, meetings and files about [project] for context. Write a plan: steps in order, who does each, the main risks, and what I should do in the first week. Say where you are assuming something the sources do not confirm."},{"u":"plan/#further","p":"Plan a piece of work","h":"Push it further","t":"","x":"Push it further The first answer is a draft. Send one of these next: Follow-up Act as a sceptical colleague. What are the three weakest assumptions? Follow-up What could go wrong in the first month, and what would I see first? Follow-up Turn this into a one-page update for my manager."},{"u":"plan/#check","p":"Plan a piece of work","h":"Before you use it","t":"","x":"Before you use it Owners and dates are suggestions until the people agree. Check assumptions against what you know that Copilot does not. Keep the plan somewhere you will update it. Results vary by licence and by your files. If a prompt misses, reply to Copilot with what to change."},{"u":"learn/","p":"Learn something fast","h":"","t":"","x":"Playbook Learn something fast Ask to be taught, then ask to be tested."},{"u":"learn/#chat","p":"Learn something fast","h":"With Copilot Chat","t":"chat","x":"With Copilot Chat Say what you know already It pitches the explanation to you. Ask for a quiz Being tested is what makes it stick. Have reading material? Put the files in a notebook and ask for a study guide, flashcards or a quiz. Try a coach agent Open Agents, then All agents, and look for Learning Coach or Prompt Coach. What you see depends on your organisation. Prompt · Chat Teach me [topic]. I already know [what you know] and I need it for [why]. Explain it in plain words with one worked example from [my kind of work]. Then ask me five questions, one at a time, and tell me what I got wrong."},{"u":"learn/#premium","p":"Learn something fast","h":"With M365 Copilot (Premium)","t":"premium","x":"With M365 Copilot (Premium) Say what you know already It pitches the explanation to you. Add how your organisation does it Ask it to use internal documents on the topic that you can open. Ask for a quiz Being tested is what makes it stick. Prompt · Premium Teach me [topic]. I already know [what you know] and I need it for [why]. Explain it in plain words, then show how we handle it here, using our internal documents on [topic]. Then ask me five questions, one at a time, and tell me what I got wrong."},{"u":"learn/#further","p":"Learn something fast","h":"Push it further","t":"","x":"Push it further The first answer is a draft. Send one of these next: Follow-up Explain that again as if I were new to the industry. Follow-up Give me a 20-minute-a-day plan for the next two weeks. Follow-up What do people most often get wrong about this?"},{"u":"learn/#check","p":"Learn something fast","h":"Before you use it","t":"","x":"Before you use it It can teach a mistake confidently. Check key facts against a trusted source. For anything regulated, your organisation's own guidance wins. If the quiz feels too easy, ask for harder questions. Results vary by licence and by your files. If a prompt misses, reply to Copilot with what to change."},{"u":"catch-up/","p":"Catch up after time away","h":"","t":"","x":"Playbook Catch up after time away An hour of scrolling, replaced by three prompts."},{"u":"catch-up/#chat","p":"Catch up after time away","h":"With Copilot Chat","t":"chat","x":"With Copilot Chat Start in Outlook Copilot there can summarise your inbox and check your calendar. Then each busy Teams chat Open the chat or channel, then ask Copilot Chat about what you have open. Make one list Paste the summaries into Copilot Chat and ask for a single to-do list. Prompt · Chat I have been away since [date]. Summarise the emails I received since then. Group them into: decisions made without me, things waiting on me, and things I can ignore. Then list my meetings for the next [three] days and what I should read before each."},{"u":"catch-up/#premium","p":"Catch up after time away","h":"With M365 Copilot (Premium)","t":"premium","x":"With M365 Copilot (Premium) Ask once Copilot can read across your emails, meetings, chats and files. Go project by project Follow up with one prompt per project for detail. Ask what is waiting on you Then do those first. Prompt · Premium I have been away since [date]. Catch me up using my emails, meetings, Teams chats and files. Give me: decisions made without me, things waiting on me, and what changed on [project]. Name the source of each point and put the most urgent first."},{"u":"catch-up/#further","p":"Catch up after time away","h":"Push it further","t":"","x":"Push it further The first answer is a draft. Send one of these next: Follow-up Draft a short \"I am back\" message to [person] that picks up the open points. Follow-up What did I miss in the [meeting name] meeting? Follow-up Which of these can I delegate?"},{"u":"catch-up/#check","p":"Catch up after time away","h":"Before you use it","t":"","x":"Before you use it Open the source for anything marked urgent. It only sees what you can open. Ask a colleague what happened off the record. Meeting answers need a transcript. See the limits. Results vary by licence and by your files. If a prompt misses, reply to Copilot with what to change."},{"u":"prompts/","p":"The prompt library","h":"","t":"","x":"Guide The prompt library Every prompt on this site in one searchable list. Fill in the blanks, copy, send."},{"u":"prompts/","p":"The prompt library","h":"The short version","t":"","x":"The short version A prompt has four parts: goal (required), context, source, expectations. Replace everything in [square brackets]. If the first result is off, say what to change instead of starting again. Search every prompt on the site, filtered to your licence. About these prompts They are built on Microsoft's four-part pattern and have not all been tested on every licence. Results differ by licence and by what is in your files, so treat each one as a starting point, fix it with a follow-up, and keep the ones that work for you."},{"u":"prompts/#library","p":"The prompt library","h":"Every prompt on this site","t":"","x":"Every prompt on this site Search by job or app, or pick a group. With My licence set at the top, you only see prompts for your licence. Open Fill in the blanks under any prompt, type your details, then copy. Search the prompts Start From Start Start I need to explain [topic or decision] to [who: my manager / a client / my team]. They know [what they already know] and care about [what they care about]. Write a first draft in under 150 words, in a friendly and direct tone. Then list the three questions they are most likely to ask. Start From Start Start Using only the document I have attached, summarise it in five bullet points for [who]. If something is not in the document, say so instead of guessing. Start From Start Start Look at my emails and meetings from the last week about [project]. Summarise what has been decided, what is still open, and anything waiting on me. Name the source of each point. The 12 moves: 1. Lead with the goal From The 12 moves Start Write a short update for [audience] so they can decide whether to [decision]. The 12 moves: 2. Say who it is for From The 12 moves Start The reader is [who]. They know [what they already know] and care most about [what they care about]. The 12 moves: 3. Hand it the source From The 12 moves Start Using only [the text below / the attached file], and nothing else, answer this: [question] The 12 moves: 4. Describe the output From The 12 moves Start Give me a table with columns [column names]. No more than [8] rows. Plain words, no jargon. The 12 moves: 5. Show an example From The 12 moves Start Here is an example of the style I want: [Paste an example here] Write the new one in the same style and length. The 12 moves: 6. Reply, don't restart From The 12 moves Start Shorter, less formal, and put the recommendation first. The 12 moves: 7. Ask for options From The 12 moves Start Give me three versions: one cautious, one direct, one persuasive. Say which you would send and why. The 12 moves: 8. Make it interview you From The 12 moves Start I want help with [task]. Ask me up to five questions, one at a time. When you have enough, write the best prompt for the job and then answer it. The 12 moves: 9. Work in steps From The 12 moves Start Step 1 only: give me an outline. Wait for my OK before you write anything else. The 12 moves: 10. Forbid guessing From The 12 moves Start If the answer is not in the source, say \"not stated\". Do not guess or fill gaps. The 12 moves: 11. Make it criticise its own work From The 12 moves Start Now act as a sceptical reviewer. List the three weakest points in what you just wrote, then fix them. The 12 moves: 12. Ask where it came from From The 12 moves Start For each claim, show where it comes from: quote the line or give the link. Mark anything you could not verify. Power features: Custom instructions to start from From Power features Start I work as [role] in [team or industry]. Use UK spelling and plain words. Lead with the answer, then the reasoning. Use short bullets for lists. If you are not sure, say so. Never guess a number, a name or a date. Power features From Power features Start Summarise this page for [who]. Give me the five points that matter, anything that looks out of date, and what I should read in full. Power features: Scheduled prompts From Power features Start Summarise what needs my attention this week from my emails, meetings and chats. List deadlines, things waiting on me, and meetings I should prepare for. Name the source of each. Clear my inbox From Clear my inbox Playbooks Summarise my unread emails from [this week] into three groups: needs a reply from me, for information only, and can wait. For each one give the sender and a one-line reason. Then name the three I should answer first, and why. Clear my inbox From Clear my inbox Playbooks Summarise my unread emails from [this week] into three groups: needs a reply from me, for information only, and can wait. For the three most urgent, check related files and Teams chats and say what I need to know before I reply. Name the source of each point. Clear my inbox: Follow-up From Clear my inbox Playbooks Draft a reply to the first one. I want to [accept / decline / ask for more time]. Under 100 words, one clear next step. Clear my inbox: Follow-up From Clear my inbox Playbooks Which of these are waiting on someone else, and since when? Clear my inbox: Follow-up From Clear my inbox Playbooks Flag the ones that can wait until Friday. Write a first draft From Write a first draft Playbooks I need to write [document type] for [audience] about [topic]. Here are my notes: [Paste your notes here] First give me an outline of five headings with one line under each. Wait for my OK before you write anything. Write a first draft From Write a first draft Playbooks Draft [document type] for [audience] about [topic], using /[file name] and my recent emails with [person]. Start with a three-line summary. Use short headings. Keep it to [one page]. Mark anything you were unsure of with the word CHECK. Write a first draft: Follow-up From Write a first draft Playbooks Rewrite the opening three ways: formal, friendly and blunt. Write a first draft: Follow-up From Write a first draft Playbooks What would a sceptical reader challenge? List the three weakest points. Write a first draft: Follow-up From Write a first draft Playbooks Cut this by a third without losing any number or date. Shorten or sharpen From Shorten or sharpen Playbooks Rewrite the text below for [audience] in about half the length. Keep every number, date and name. Use plain words and short sentences. After the rewrite, list anything you removed that they might still ask about. [Paste the text here] Shorten or sharpen From Shorten or sharpen Playbooks Rewrite the selected text for [audience] in about half the length. Keep every number, date and name. Use plain words and short sentences. Then list anything you removed that they might still ask about. Shorten or sharpen: Follow-up From Shorten or sharpen Playbooks Give me three versions: neutral, warmer, firmer. Shorten or sharpen: Follow-up From Shorten or sharpen Playbooks Turn this into five bullet points for a slide. Shorten or sharpen: Follow-up From Shorten or sharpen Playbooks What is unclear or ambiguous in this text? Quote the phrase. Summarise a long document From Summarise a long document Playbooks Using only the attached document, summarise it for [who], who must decide [what]. Give me: the three most important points, the risks or open questions, and what is missing from the document. If something is not in the document, say so. Do not guess. Summarise a long document From Summarise a long document Playbooks Using only /[file name], summarise it for [who], who must decide [what]. Give me: the three most important points, the risks or open questions, and what is missing from the document. If something is not in the document, say so. Do not guess. Summarise a long document: Follow-up From Summarise a long document Playbooks List every date, number and commitment in a table, with the page or section. Summarise a long document: Follow-up From Summarise a long document Playbooks What are the three weakest arguments in this document? Summarise a long document: Follow-up From Summarise a long document Playbooks Write five questions I should ask the author. Prepare for a meeting From Prepare for a meeting Playbooks I have a [30]-minute meeting with [who] about [topic]. My goal is [outcome]. Background: [Paste any background here] Give me a timed agenda, three questions I should ask, and two objections I should be ready for. Keep it to one page. Prepare for a meeting From Prepare for a meeting Playbooks Prepare me for my meeting with [person] on [day]. Use our recent emails, chats and shared files. Give me: a one-line goal, where we left things, three questions to ask, two objections to expect, and anything I promised and have not done. Name the source of each point. Prepare for a meeting: Follow-up From Prepare for a meeting Playbooks Draft a two-line message I can send beforehand to set the agenda. Prepare for a meeting: Follow-up From Prepare for a meeting Playbooks Play the other side. What will they want from this meeting? Prepare for a meeting: Follow-up From Prepare for a meeting Playbooks What decision should we leave the room with? Follow up a meeting From Follow up a meeting Playbooks Below are the notes from our meeting. Write follow-up notes for the people who attended. Include decisions, action items as a table (action, owner, due date), and open questions. Use only what is in the notes. Write \"not stated\" if an owner or date is missing. [Paste the notes here] Follow up a meeting From Follow up a meeting Playbooks Write follow-up notes for the people who attended this meeting. Include decisions, action items as a table (action, owner, due date), and open questions. Use only what was said. Write \"not stated\" if an owner or date is missing, and quote the line each action came from. Follow up a meeting: Follow-up From Follow up a meeting Playbooks Draft the email that sends these notes. Three lines, then the table. Follow up a meeting: Follow-up From Follow up a meeting Playbooks What was discussed but not decided? Follow up a meeting: Follow-up From Follow up a meeting Playbooks Which actions are mine? Make sense of a spreadsheet From Make sense of a spreadsheet Playbooks Here is a table of data: [Paste the data here] Tell me three things that stand out and one thing that looks like an error or an outlier. Explain how you know. Then write a formula for column [D] that [describes what it should calculate], and explain it in one sentence. Make sense of a spreadsheet From Make sense of a spreadsheet Playbooks Look at the data on this sheet. Tell me three things that stand out and one thing that looks like an error or an outlier. Then show me your plan for a summary table of [measure] by [category] and [month], with a chart of the trend. Use formulas, not pasted values. Do not change anything until I say so. Make sense of a spreadsheet: Follow-up From Make sense of a spreadsheet Playbooks Explain this formula step by step: [Paste the formula here] Make sense of a spreadsheet: Follow-up From Make sense of a spreadsheet Playbooks What happens to the result if a cell in column [B] is empty? Make sense of a spreadsheet: Follow-up From Make sense of a spreadsheet Playbooks Which rows did you exclude, and why? Build a deck From Build a deck Playbooks Draft an outline for an [8]-slide presentation for [audience] about [topic]. Purpose: help them decide [decision]. For each slide give a title that makes one point, three short bullets, and one suggested visual. Use only the notes below. [Paste your notes here] Build a deck From Build a deck Playbooks Create an [8]-slide presentation for [audience] about [topic]. Purpose: help them decide [decision]. Structure: the problem, three options with pros and cons, a recommendation, next steps. One idea per slide. Keep slide text short. Show me the outline before you build it. Build a deck: Follow-up From Build a deck Playbooks Rewrite every slide title so it states the conclusion. Build a deck: Follow-up From Build a deck Playbooks Write speaker notes for slide [3], about 60 seconds long. Build a deck: Follow-up From Build a deck Playbooks What questions will this audience ask? Add a backup slide for the hardest one. Research a topic From Research a topic Playbooks Research [topic] using public web sources, for a decision on [decision]. Give me the key findings, where sources disagree, the risks, and a recommendation. Cite every claim with its link. Mark anything you could not verify. Research a topic From Research a topic Playbooks Research [topic] for a decision on [decision]. Scope: [the web only / our internal documents and emails only / both]. I need the key findings, where sources disagree, the risks, and a recommendation. Cite every claim. Mark anything you could not verify. Research a topic: Follow-up From Research a topic Playbooks What is the strongest argument against your recommendation? Research a topic: Follow-up From Research a topic Playbooks Which of these sources is least reliable, and why? Research a topic: Follow-up From Research a topic Playbooks Summarise this in five lines for someone senior. Plan a piece of work From Plan a piece of work Playbooks I need to plan [piece of work]. The deadline is [date] and the people involved are [who]. Ask me up to five questions, one at a time, to understand it. Then write a plan: steps in order, who does each, the main risks, and what I should do in the first week. Plan a piece of work From Plan a piece of work Playbooks I need to plan [piece of work]. Use my emails, meetings and files about [project] for context. Write a plan: steps in order, who does each, the main risks, and what I should do in the first week. Say where you are assuming something the sources do not confirm. Plan a piece of work: Follow-up From Plan a piece of work Playbooks Act as a sceptical colleague. What are the three weakest assumptions? Plan a piece of work: Follow-up From Plan a piece of work Playbooks What could go wrong in the first month, and what would I see first? Plan a piece of work: Follow-up From Plan a piece of work Playbooks Turn this into a one-page update for my manager. Learn something fast From Learn something fast Playbooks Teach me [topic]. I already know [what you know] and I need it for [why]. Explain it in plain words with one worked example from [my kind of work]. Then ask me five questions, one at a time, and tell me what I got wrong. Learn something fast From Learn something fast Playbooks Teach me [topic]. I already know [what you know] and I need it for [why]. Explain it in plain words, then show how we handle it here, using our internal documents on [topic]. Then ask me five questions, one at a time, and tell me what I got wrong. Learn something fast: Follow-up From Learn something fast Playbooks Explain that again as if I were new to the industry. Learn something fast: Follow-up From Learn something fast Playbooks Give me a 20-minute-a-day plan for the next two weeks. Learn something fast: Follow-up From Learn something fast Playbooks What do people most often get wrong about this? Catch up after time away From Catch up after time away Playbooks I have been away since [date]. Summarise the emails I received since then. Group them into: decisions made without me, things waiting on me, and things I can ignore. Then list my meetings for the next [three] days and what I should read before each. Catch up after time away From Catch up after time away Playbooks I have been away since [date]. Catch me up using my emails, meetings, Teams chats and files. Give me: decisions made without me, things waiting on me, and what changed on [project]. Name the source of each point and put the most urgent first. Catch up after time away: Follow-up From Catch up after time away Playbooks Draft a short \"I am back\" message to [person] that picks up the open points. Catch up after time away: Follow-up From Catch up after time away Playbooks What did I miss in the [meeting name] meeting? Catch up after time away: Follow-up From Catch up after time away Playbooks Which of these can I delegate? Reply to a difficult email Everyday Draft a reply to the email below. Context: the sender is [role], and I want to [agree / decline / buy time] without sounding defensive. Keep it under 120 words, polite and direct, and end with one clear next step. [Paste the email here] Make something shorter without losing the point Everyday Rewrite the text below for [audience] in about half the length. Keep every number and date. Use plain words and short sentences. After the rewrite, list anything you removed that they might still ask about. [Paste the text here] Summarise an attached document for a decision Everyday Using only the attached document, summarise it for a busy manager who must decide [what]. Give me: the three most important points, any risks or open questions, and what is missing from the document. If something is not in the document, say so rather than guessing. Turn notes into actions Everyday From these notes, list the action items as a table with columns: action, owner, due date. If an owner or date is not stated, write \"not stated\" rather than inventing one. [Paste the notes here] Prepare for a meeting Everyday I have a [length]-minute meeting with [who] about [topic]. My goal is [outcome]. Suggest an agenda with timings, three questions I should ask, and two objections I should be ready for. Keep it to one page. Challenge my plan Everyday Here is my plan: [paste or describe it]. Act as a sceptical colleague. Tell me the three weakest assumptions, what could go wrong in the first month, and one thing I haven't considered. Be specific, not generic. When you don't know what to ask Everyday I want help with [task] but I'm not sure how to ask. Ask me up to five questions, one at a time, to understand what I need. When you have enough, write the best prompt for the job and then answer it. Reply to a difficult email Everyday Draft a reply to the open email from [name]. Context: I want to [agree / decline / buy time] without sounding defensive. Check our earlier emails on this so the reply is consistent. Keep it under 120 words, polite and direct, and end with one clear next step. Summarise a file for a decision Everyday Using only /[file name], summarise it for a busy manager who must decide [what]. Give me: the three most important points, any risks or open questions, and what is missing from the document. If something is not in the document, say so rather than guessing. Catch me up on a project Everyday Catch me up on [project] from the last [two weeks]: key decisions, what is blocked, and what is waiting on me. Use my emails, meetings and files. Name the source for each point. Prepare for a meeting Everyday I have a [length]-minute meeting with [who] about [topic]. My goal is [outcome]. Use our recent emails and shared files to suggest an agenda with timings, three questions I should ask, and two objections I should be ready for. Keep it to one page. Turn meeting notes into actions Everyday From the notes in /[file name], list the action items as a table with columns: action, owner, due date. If an owner or date is not stated, write \"not stated\" rather than inventing one. Challenge my plan Everyday Here is my plan: /[file name]. Act as a sceptical colleague. Tell me the three weakest assumptions, what could go wrong in the first month, and one thing I haven't considered. Be specific, not generic. Copilot Chat: What to expect From Copilot Chat Copilot Chat Using only the text I have pasted below, list the decisions made and the open questions. If something is not in the text, say so. [Paste the text here] Copilot Chat: Work data, automatically From Copilot Chat Copilot Chat Find the latest version of the [project name] proposal and the email thread about it. Summarise where we have landed and what is still open. Link to the sources you used. Word/Outlook: Word From Word/Outlook Word/Outlook Rewrite the text below for a [client / executive / new starter] audience. Keep it factual and under [80] words. Keep all dates and figures unchanged. [Paste the text here] Word/Outlook: Outlook From Word/Outlook Word/Outlook Summarise my unread emails from this week into: needs a reply from me, for information only, and can wait. List the sender and a one-line reason for each. Word/Outlook: Outlook From Word/Outlook Word/Outlook Draft a reply to the open email. I want to [accept / decline / ask for more time]. Keep it under 100 words, polite and direct, with one clear next step. Word/Outlook: Word From Word/Outlook Word/Outlook Draft a one-page briefing on [topic] for [audience], using /[file name] and my recent emails with [person]. Keep it factual, with a short summary at the top and next steps at the end. Word/Outlook: Outlook From Word/Outlook Word/Outlook Prepare me for my meeting with [person] tomorrow. Use our recent emails and shared files. Give me a short agenda, the last three decisions we made, and anything outstanding. Excel From Excel Excel Here is a table of data: [paste it]. Write a formula for column [D] that [describes what it should calculate]. Explain it in one sentence, and tell me what happens if a cell in [B] is empty. Excel From Excel Excel Look at the pasted data. Tell me three things that stand out, and one thing that looks like an error or an outlier. Explain how you know. [Paste the data here] Excel From Excel Excel Look at the data on this sheet. Tell me three things that stand out, and one thing that looks like an error or an outlier. Explain how you know. Don't change anything. Excel From Excel Excel I want a summary table of [measure] by [category] and [month], plus a chart that shows the trend. Show me your plan first. Use formulas, not pasted values, so the table updates when the data does. PowerPoint From PowerPoint PowerPoint Draft an outline for an [8]-slide presentation for [audience] about [topic]. Purpose: help them decide [decision]. For each slide give a title, three short bullet points and a suggestion for one visual. Use only the notes I have pasted below. [Paste your notes here] PowerPoint: Turning a Word document into a deck From PowerPoint PowerPoint Create a [8]-slide presentation for [audience] about [topic]. Purpose: help them decide [decision]. Structure: the problem, three options with pros and cons, a recommendation, and next steps. Tone: plain and confident. One main idea per slide, and keep slide text short. Teams From Teams Teams What has been decided so far, and what is still open? List any action items with the person responsible, if one was named. Teams From Teams Teams Write follow-up notes for the people who attended. Include decisions, action items (owner and date if mentioned), and open questions. Use only what was said in the meeting. If something is unclear, flag it instead of filling it in. Teams From Teams Teams Below are the notes from our meeting. Write follow-up notes for the people who attended. Include decisions, action items (owner and date if mentioned), and open questions. Use only what is in the notes. If something is unclear, flag it instead of filling it in. [Paste the notes here] Teams From Teams Teams Summarise the meeting for someone who missed it. Give the decisions, the action items with owners, and the open questions. Quote the part of the discussion each one came from. Researcher From Researcher Agents Research [topic] using public web sources for a decision on [decision]. Give me the key findings, where sources disagree, risks, and a recommendation. Cite every claim with its link. Mark anything you could not verify. Researcher From Researcher Agents Research [topic] for a decision on [decision]. Scope: [the web only / our internal documents and emails only / both]. I need: the key findings, where sources disagree, risks, and a recommendation. Cite every claim. Mark anything you could not verify. Analyst From Analyst Agents Here is a table: [paste it]. What are the three biggest trends, and one number that doesn't fit? Explain how you calculated each, and say what you could not tell from this data. Analyst From Analyst Agents Here is my data: [attach file]. What are the three biggest trends, and one number that doesn't fit? Show a chart for each trend and explain how you calculated it. Other agents: Visual Creator From Other agents Agents Create a simple visual that explains [idea] to [audience]. Style: clean and minimal, using [two colours]. Add a short caption. Other agents: Idea Coach From Other agents Agents I need ideas for [goal]. Give me five, then help me pick one and turn it into a three-step plan with a first action for this week. Other agents: Writing Coach From Other agents Agents Review the text below for tone, grammar and clarity for [audience]. Point out the three changes that would help most, and explain why. [Paste the text here] Other agents From Other agents Agents Here is a prompt I use: [paste it]. Improve it using goal, context, source and expectations, and explain each change. Other agents: Learning Coach From Other agents Agents I want to learn [skill] in [four weeks], at [30 minutes a day]. Build me a study plan with a weekly goal and a short quiz at the end of each week. Other agents: Career Coach From Other agents Agents My current role is [role]. I want to move towards [goal]. List the skills I should build, in order, and one small step for each. Make your own: Instruction template From Make your own Agents Role: You are [role] for [team/audience]. Purpose: Help people with [the jobs it should do]. Use only: [the knowledge sources]. If the answer isn't there, say \"I don't know\" and say who to ask. Format: [short answers / bullets / steps]. Keep a [friendly, plain] tone. Never: [give legal or HR advice / guess / share personal data]. Always: end with the source you used. Make your own From Make your own Agents You are a plain-English explainer for [team]. Use only the websites listed as knowledge. Answer in five lines or fewer, link the page you used, and say \"I don't know\" if the sites don't say. Make your own From Make your own Agents You are the project assistant for [project]. Use the SharePoint folder and the project's Teams meetings as knowledge. Answer questions on decisions, owners and next steps. Quote the source, and say when the sources are silent. Agent prompts: Researcher From Agent prompts Agents Research [topic] for a decision on [decision]. Scope: [web only / work sources / both]. Give key findings, where sources disagree, risks and a recommendation. Cite every claim and mark anything unverified. Agent prompts: Analyst From Agent prompts Agents Here is my data: [attach file]. What are the three biggest trends, and one number that doesn't fit? Show a chart for each and explain the calculation. Agent prompts: Team FAQ agent From Agent prompts Agents You are the team assistant for [team]. Answer questions about how we work using only the knowledge sources. Keep answers to five lines. Name the document you used. If it isn't covered, say \"I don't know\" and name [person] to ask. Agent prompts: Onboarding buddy From Agent prompts Agents You help new starters in [team] in their first 30 days. Use only the onboarding documents. Answer in plain words, give steps in order, and finish each answer with \"What would you like to know next?\" Never give HR, legal or pay advice; point to [person] instead. Agent prompts: Writing-style checker From Agent prompts Agents You check text against our style guide (in the knowledge source). For any text I paste, list up to five changes that would bring it in line, quote the rule for each, then give a corrected version. Do not change facts, figures or names. Agent prompts: Meeting prep From Agent prompts Agents You prepare me for meetings. When I give a meeting topic and attendees, produce: a one-line goal, a timed agenda, three questions to ask, and two likely objections. Use only the project documents in the knowledge source. Say what is missing. Agent prompts: Policy explainer From Agent prompts Agents You explain our policies in plain English using only the policy documents in the knowledge source. Give the short answer first, then the policy wording you relied on and its document name. You are not a decision-maker. For exceptions or doubt, say who to ask. Never guess."},{"u":"prompts/#recipe","p":"The prompt library","h":"The recipe","t":"","x":"The recipe Part What to say Required? Goal The task you want done Yes Context Why you need it and who it is for No Source What to draw on: your own pasted text, a file, or a web page No Expectations Format, tone, length No"},{"u":"prompts/#habit","p":"The prompt library","h":"Habits worth keeping","t":"","x":"Habits worth keeping Instruct it not to guess. \"If it's not in the document, say so\" works better than hoping. Fix, don't restart. \"Shorter, and less formal\" is a complete follow-up. Don't paste what you shouldn't. Read Safety first."},{"u":"apps/","p":"App guides","h":"","t":"","x":"App guides Copilot, app by app The detail behind the playbooks: where the buttons are, what each mode does, and the limits Microsoft lists. Copilot Chat Getting in, giving it files, choosing how it thinks. Word and Outlook Drafting, rewriting and email. Excel Three modes, which devices can edit, and how to check its work. PowerPoint Decks from a prompt or a Word file, and the limits. Teams meetings The organiser's setting, recaps and limits. Looking for a job, not an app? Go to the playbooks."},{"u":"copilot-chat/","p":"Copilot Chat","h":"","t":"","x":"App guide Copilot Chat The everyday chat. It is the one part of Copilot almost everyone at a Microsoft 365 company already has."},{"u":"copilot-chat/","p":"Copilot Chat","h":"The short version","t":"","x":"The short version Open it at copilot.cloud.microsoft, in the Edge sidebar, or inside Outlook and Teams, signed in with your work account. It answers from the web. To use a work document you give it that document. It can search your work data for you. Check anything important. It can be wrong, and it can answer differently each time."},{"u":"copilot-chat/#get-in","p":"Copilot Chat","h":"Getting in","t":"","x":"Getting in Copilot Chat is available to people who sign in with a Microsoft Entra work or school account on an eligible Microsoft 365 plan, at no extra cost. You can reach it in several places: The Microsoft Copilot app on web, desktop and mobile ( copilot.cloud.microsoft) The Copilot icon in the Edge sidebar, when Edge is signed in with your work account Copilot Chat inside Outlook and Teams Check which account you are in The app also accepts personal Microsoft accounts. Chats, files and protections are kept separate for each. For work, look for the Work label under your profile and the green data protection shield. Your organisation can turn web search off with the Allow web search in Copilot policy. If it does, Copilot answers from the language model alone. That is an organisation setting, not something you can change."},{"u":"copilot-chat/#models","p":"Copilot Chat","h":"Choosing how it thinks","t":"","x":"Choosing how it thinks Copilot has a model selector. The default is Auto, which picks for you. Option Use it for Auto Most of the time Quick response Simple questions where you want a short answer fast Think deeper Open-ended or complex questions where you would rather wait for a more thorough answer Microsoft notes these options change as new models arrive. Whether models from outside providers, such as Anthropic and OpenAI, appear is an organisation setting."},{"u":"copilot-chat/#habits","p":"Copilot Chat","h":"Four habits that help","t":"","x":"Four habits that help Start with the goal. Add context, sources and the format you want only if you need them. Follow up. Microsoft expects you to refine; the first answer is a draft. Check what matters. Microsoft says responses are not guaranteed to be fully factual and you should review them before sending them to others. Run it twice if it matters. The same prompt can give different results because of built-in randomness."},{"u":"copilot-chat/#chat","p":"Copilot Chat","h":"With Copilot Chat","t":"chat","x":"With Copilot Chat Giving it your files Chat cannot search Microsoft Graph, so it does not look through your emails, files and meetings for you. Microsoft says there are three ways to get work content into it: Paste or upload it Copy text into the prompt, upload a file, or select a file when you write the prompt. Open it first In Outlook and Teams, Copilot Chat can see the item you have open, so you do not need to paste it. In Outlook it can also work with your inbox and calendar (see Word and Outlook). Use an agent Pay-as-you-go agents can reach organisational content, if your organisation has turned this on. See Agents. What to expect The web: when Copilot decides a search would help, it sends a query to Bing and answers with public information. Your work data: only what you provide. Prompt · Chat Using only the text I have pasted below, list the decisions made and the open questions. If something is not in the text, say so. [Paste the text here]"},{"u":"copilot-chat/#premium","p":"Copilot Chat","h":"With M365 Copilot (Premium)","t":"premium","x":"With M365 Copilot (Premium) Work data, automatically The same chat, but Copilot can use your emails, files, meetings, chats and calendar through Microsoft Graph, with no upload needed. It also searches the web when it decides that would help. You can name a file to focus on, but you do not have to upload it. It shows you only what you already have at least view permission to open. A file you cannot open will not appear. If answers are not using your work context, Work IQ may be switched off for your organisation. That is an organisation setting; see Troubleshooting. Prompt · Premium Find the latest version of the [project name] proposal and the email thread about it. Summarise where we have landed and what is still open. Link to the sources you used. Ready-made starting points are in the prompt library."},{"u":"word-outlook/","p":"Word and Outlook","h":"","t":"","x":"App guide Word and Outlook Writing is where most people first get value. Both apps have Copilot, but they behave differently depending on your tier."},{"u":"word-outlook/","p":"Word and Outlook","h":"The short version","t":"","x":"The short version Word: describe what you want, optionally point to a source file, then keep, refine or discard the draft. Outlook: Copilot Chat sits next to your inbox and can work with your inbox and calendar on both tracks. Everything it writes is a draft. Check names, numbers, dates and anything you would be embarrassed to get wrong."},{"u":"word-outlook/#shared","p":"Word and Outlook","h":"For both tracks: check before you send","t":"","x":"For both tracks: check before you send Check before you send Microsoft's own list of things to verify: facts, numbers, citations, names, policy statements and other sensitive details. Copilot also applies the document's sensitivity label. Some features roll out gradually and depend on your licence, your organisation's settings, your app version and your platform. If a button in a screenshot is not on your screen, that is the usual reason. Drafting an email is not sending one: read it, then send it yourself."},{"u":"word-outlook/#chat","p":"Word and Outlook","h":"With Copilot Chat","t":"chat","x":"With Copilot Chat Word Microsoft says the Copilot Chat (Basic) label has no Copilot inside Word, while M365 Copilot (Basic) has standard access. Open Word and look for the Copilot icon at the bottom right to see which you have. If you do not have it, you can still use Chat in your browser: paste the paragraph or upload the file, and ask for a rewrite. Prompt · Chat Rewrite the text below for a [client / executive / new starter] audience. Keep it factual and under [80] words. Keep all dates and figures unchanged. [Paste the text here] Outlook In Outlook, Copilot is a chat panel beside your mail. Sign in with your work or school account and select the Copilot button near the top of the app. Microsoft's Outlook support page lists Outlook for Microsoft 365, Outlook for Mac and New Outlook for Windows. Its Learn requirements page says Copilot works with \"supported versions\" of classic Outlook too, and needs an Exchange Online mailbox; Copilot isn't available in group mailboxes. Microsoft doesn't list which classic builds count, so the test is whether your Outlook shows the Copilot button. On the Chat track, Copilot in Outlook can answer questions about your inbox, calendar and meetings and other limited data. It can take actions in Outlook: summarise emails, check meeting times, draft emails, schedule meetings, flag emails and set up automatic replies. Prompt · Chat Summarise my unread emails from this week into: needs a reply from me, for information only, and can wait. List the sender and a one-line reason for each. Prompt · Chat Draft a reply to the open email. I want to [accept / decline / ask for more time]. Keep it under 100 words, polite and direct, with one clear next step."},{"u":"word-outlook/#premium","p":"Word and Outlook","h":"With M365 Copilot (Premium)","t":"premium","x":"With M365 Copilot (Premium) Word Copilot in Word can draft from a request, an outline, notes or reference files; reuse the formatting of an existing template; add sections or tables; rewrite selected text; and add citations to the sources it used. Place the cursor Open a blank document, or click where the new text should go. Open Copilot and describe the job Click the Copilot icon at the bottom right. Say the topic, audience, tone, format and length. Add a source, if you want one Type / to choose a file Copilot should use. Generate, then decide Keep it, discard it, regenerate it, or refine it with a follow-up prompt. To edit what you already have, select text and ask Copilot to rewrite it, change the tone, shorten it or offer alternatives. You can also restructure a document in place with Edit with Copilot. Prompt · Premium Draft a one-page briefing on [topic] for [audience], using /[file name] and my recent emails with [person]. Keep it factual, with a short summary at the top and next steps at the end. Outlook Everything in the Chat track for Outlook, plus your chats and enterprise data such as documents. Microsoft's examples include prioritising unread email and drafting meeting agendas or pre-reads. The same version and mailbox requirements apply. Prompt · Premium Prepare me for my meeting with [person] tomorrow. Use our recent emails and shared files. Give me a short agenda, the last three decisions we made, and anything outstanding. If a button is missing, see Troubleshooting. More prompts are in the prompt library."},{"u":"excel/","p":"Excel","h":"","t":"","x":"App guide Excel Copilot in Excel can change your workbook, not just talk about it. That makes the mode you pick matter."},{"u":"excel/","p":"Excel","h":"The short version","t":"","x":"The short version There are three modes: Allow editing, Plan and Chat only. Use Plan or Chat only when the file matters. It uses Excel's own features (formulas, charts, PivotTables), so what it makes stays editable. Editing works on Windows, Mac and the web. Phones are chat only for now. Keep a copy before a big multi-step change."},{"u":"excel/#can","p":"Excel","h":"What it can do","t":"","x":"What it can do According to Microsoft, Copilot in Excel can: Add, rename and delete sheets, change cells and ranges, and apply formatting, validation and layouts. Generate data and formulas across sheets. Create charts, PivotTables and shapes. Answer questions about your data, and sort and filter. Search the web and cite sources. Import data from other workbooks (OneDrive, SharePoint or your computer). Run saved \"skills\" for repeatable tasks. Microsoft says it updates your workbook using Excel's built-in features, so the content stays editable. A Stop button lets you halt a task that is running."},{"u":"excel/#modes","p":"Excel","h":"The three modes","t":"","x":"The three modes Mode What happens Use it when Allow editing (default) Handles multi-step tasks and edits the workbook directly A throwaway file, or you've saved a copy Plan Proposes a plan for you to review before any change A file other people rely on Chat only Analyses and answers; does not modify the workbook You only want an explanation or a formula to copy The page does not use the phrase \"Agent mode\"; Microsoft's other pages and blog posts do."},{"u":"excel/#where","p":"Excel","h":"Where it works","t":"","x":"Where it works Platform Modes available Windows, Mac, web Edit, chat and plan iPad Chat and plan (editing is rolling out) iPhone Chat only (editing is rolling out) Android Chat only Access depends on having an eligible Copilot licence and on your organisation's settings."},{"u":"excel/#unknown","p":"Excel","h":"What Microsoft's page doesn't say","t":"","x":"What Microsoft's page doesn't say What Microsoft doesn't say The Excel support page does not say whether your data must be formatted as a table, how large a workbook can be, which functions are supported, or how to undo a Copilot change. So work on a copy. If Copilot refuses a big workbook, split the data or try a smaller range."},{"u":"excel/#check","p":"Excel","h":"Check its work","t":"","x":"Check its work Click into a few generated cells and read the formulas. Do they refer to the right ranges? Spot-check one total by hand. Be most careful when it filters or sorts. Make sure rows weren't dropped."},{"u":"excel/#chat","p":"Excel","h":"With Copilot Chat","t":"chat","x":"With Copilot Chat If your Excel has no Copilot pane, you can still use Chat in the browser: paste a small table, or upload the file, and ask for a formula or an explanation. Chat does not change your workbook. Do not paste data your organisation does not allow you to share; see Safety. Prompt · Chat Here is a table of data: [paste it]. Write a formula for column [D] that [describes what it should calculate]. Explain it in one sentence, and tell me what happens if a cell in [B] is empty. Prompt · Chat Look at the pasted data. Tell me three things that stand out, and one thing that looks like an error or an outlier. Explain how you know. [Paste the data here]"},{"u":"excel/#premium","p":"Excel","h":"With M365 Copilot (Premium)","t":"premium","x":"With M365 Copilot (Premium) Copilot works inside the workbook, in the mode you choose, and can import data from other workbooks you can open. Pick the mode in the Copilot pane. Need a different kind of analysis? Look at the Analyst agent on its page. Prompt · Premium, Chat only mode Look at the data on this sheet. Tell me three things that stand out, and one thing that looks like an error or an outlier. Explain how you know. Don't change anything. Prompt · Premium, Plan mode I want a summary table of [measure] by [category] and [month], plus a chart that shows the trend. Show me your plan first. Use formulas, not pasted values, so the table updates when the data does."},{"u":"powerpoint/","p":"PowerPoint","h":"","t":"","x":"App guide PowerPoint Copilot can give you a working first deck in minutes. Treat it as a draft you shape, not a finished product."},{"u":"powerpoint/","p":"PowerPoint","h":"The short version","t":"","x":"The short version Start from a prompt or from a Word document. Chat and Premium tracks differ in where you start. It may ask about audience and style, then show an outline before building slides. Start from your company template. Microsoft says Word source files work best under 24 MB. Edit and review. Microsoft says AI output needs a person to check it."},{"u":"powerpoint/#chat","p":"PowerPoint","h":"With Copilot Chat","t":"chat","x":"With Copilot Chat Microsoft says the Copilot Chat (Basic) label has no Copilot inside PowerPoint, and its PowerPoint page says Copilot may not be included with your subscription or your organisation's settings. What you can do on the Chat track is use Chat to prepare the content, then build the slides yourself. Prompt · Chat Draft an outline for an [8]-slide presentation for [audience] about [topic]. Purpose: help them decide [decision]. For each slide give a title, three short bullet points and a suggestion for one visual. Use only the notes I have pasted below. [Paste your notes here] To check what you have, open PowerPoint and look for the Copilot icon at the bottom right. If it is not there, see Troubleshooting."},{"u":"powerpoint/#premium","p":"PowerPoint","h":"With M365 Copilot (Premium)","t":"premium","x":"With M365 Copilot (Premium) Inside PowerPoint Open a new presentation Ideally from your organisation's template. Open Copilot Click the Copilot icon at the bottom right, then Add Content. Choose Agent Mode and enter your prompt Copilot may ask clarifying questions about audience and style, and builds an outline you can refine before it creates the slides. In the Copilot app Sign in at m365.cloud.microsoft with your work or school account, ask Copilot to create a presentation on your topic, and confirm when prompted. It saves the deck to your OneDrive and gives you a link. Turning a Word document into a deck Copilot can pull a Word document's structure and images into slides. Microsoft's tips: Microsoft says it works best with Word documents smaller than 24 MB. That is a tip, not a stated hard limit. Starting from your organisation's template gives the best results. You need access and permission to use the file you point it to. Prompt · Premium Create a [8]-slide presentation for [audience] about [topic]. Purpose: help them decide [decision]. Structure: the problem, three options with pros and cons, a recommendation, and next steps. Tone: plain and confident. One main idea per slide, and keep slide text short."},{"u":"powerpoint/#limits","p":"PowerPoint","h":"Limits Microsoft lists","t":"","x":"Limits Microsoft lists Expect one output language per deck. Microsoft's page listed that limit in October; it was not on the page when re-checked in November. When you create from a file, you can't add extra context in the same prompt. Referencing files in Agent Mode is listed as \"coming soon\". Controls for length, tone, style and image preferences are rolling out first to Frontier and Microsoft 365 Insiders, then to all Copilot subscribers later. AI-generated images are labelled \"AI-generated\" in the slide's speaker notes. Licence Copilot in PowerPoint needs to be included with your subscription, and your organisation's settings must allow it. To check what you have, start with Start here."},{"u":"powerpoint/#review","p":"PowerPoint","h":"Before you present","t":"","x":"Before you present Read every slide for facts and numbers. Don't trust generated statistics. Check images: do they fit your brand and your audience? Cut. First drafts almost always have too much text."},{"u":"teams-meetings/","p":"Teams meetings","h":"","t":"","x":"App guide Teams meetings Copilot can summarise a meeting while it happens and after it ends. Whether it can depends mostly on a setting the organiser chose."},{"u":"teams-meetings/","p":"Teams meetings","h":"The short version","t":"","x":"The short version The organiser controls Copilot for their meeting. If it's off, nobody can turn it on. To ask questions after the meeting, transcription must have been on. It doesn't work in meetings hosted by another organisation. Check summaries against what you remember before you act on them or forward them."},{"u":"teams-meetings/#setting","p":"Teams meetings","h":"The organiser's setting","t":"","x":"The organiser's setting In Meeting options, under Copilot and other AI, the organiser picks one of three choices: Choice What it means During and after the meeting Transcription is required. Copilot starts and stops with transcription, and works afterwards. Only during the meeting Copilot uses speech-to-text and is available only live. Untranscribed speech and Copilot chats are not available after the meeting ends. Off Copilot, recording and transcription are all disabled. If you organise meetings, pick deliberately. If you attend them and want recaps, you can suggest to the organiser that they choose the first option. Which choices are available can also depend on your company's policy, sensitivity labels and meeting templates."},{"u":"teams-meetings/#during","p":"Teams meetings","h":"During the meeting","t":"","x":"During the meeting Copilot can summarise key discussion points, note who said what, suggest action items and answer questions in real time. You talk to it in a private chat. Other attendees do not see your questions. Prompt suggestions appear under View prompts. If you join more than 5 minutes late and Copilot is active, it offers a catch-up summary. Prompt What has been decided so far, and what is still open? List any action items with the person responsible, if one was named."},{"u":"teams-meetings/#after","p":"Teams meetings","h":"After the meeting","t":"","x":"After the meeting Open Copilot from the meeting chat or the Recap tab. Copilot cites its sources. Long answers (over 1,300 characters) can open in Word, and tables can open in Excel. Prompt Write follow-up notes for the people who attended. Include decisions, action items (owner and date if mentioned), and open questions. Use only what was said in the meeting. If something is unclear, flag it instead of filling it in."},{"u":"teams-meetings/#limits","p":"Teams meetings","h":"Limits Microsoft lists","t":"","x":"Limits Microsoft lists Doesn't work in meetings hosted outside your organisation. After-meeting questions about spoken content need a transcript. In a recurring meeting, earlier Copilot conversation history is lost if a later meeting in the series is transcribed. If the transcript is gone, Copilot is unavailable and history is removed. Export to Word or Excel can be blocked by a sensitivity label or by the option that turns off copying and forwarding of chat, captions and transcripts. Intelligent recap needs a Teams Premium add-on or a Copilot licence. Be careful with people's words Summaries can misattribute or soften what someone said. Check anything that affects a person, a decision or a customer. Microsoft also restricts Copilot from judging an employee's performance or emotional state from workplace communication."},{"u":"teams-meetings/#chat","p":"Teams meetings","h":"With Copilot Chat","t":"chat","x":"With Copilot Chat In Teams, Copilot Chat can see the meeting or item you have open, so you do not need to paste it. Whether the meeting copilot (summaries, live questions, recap) is available to you depends on your tier and on the organiser's setting above. Intelligent recap needs a Teams Premium add-on or a Copilot licence, so on the Chat track expect the recap to be missing unless Teams Premium is on. What always works: copy the transcript or notes from the meeting chat and paste them into Copilot Chat. Prompt · Chat Below are the notes from our meeting. Write follow-up notes for the people who attended. Include decisions, action items (owner and date if mentioned), and open questions. Use only what is in the notes. If something is unclear, flag it instead of filling it in. [Paste the notes here]"},{"u":"teams-meetings/#premium","p":"Teams meetings","h":"With M365 Copilot (Premium)","t":"premium","x":"With M365 Copilot (Premium) Copilot works in the meeting itself and from the meeting chat or Recap tab afterwards, within the organiser's setting. Use the prompts in the During and After sections above. Prompt · Premium Summarise the meeting for someone who missed it. Give the decisions, the action items with owners, and the open questions. Quote the part of the discussion each one came from. The Teams page shows no update date. Symptoms and fixes are in Troubleshooting."},{"u":"agents/","p":"Agents: the overview","h":"","t":"","x":"Agents Agents: the overview An agent is Copilot set up for one kind of job. You pick it, then give it the task."},{"u":"agents/","p":"Agents: the overview","h":"The short version","t":"","x":"The short version Microsoft ships a set of agents, and you can build your own. This section covers agents in Microsoft 365. It does not cover Copilot Studio. Find agents in the Agents area of the Copilot app, or type @ and pick one. You get agents grounded in instructions and the web. Agents that use work data are pay-as-you-go and must be switched on by your organisation. You also get Researcher, Analyst and agents that can use your email, meetings and files."},{"u":"agents/#what","p":"Agents: the overview","h":"What an agent is","t":"","x":"What an agent is Microsoft describes agents as scoped or focused versions of Copilot that act as assistants and can automate processes. An agent is a set of instructions, plus optional knowledge sources such as websites or files, plus the tools it may use."},{"u":"agents/#find","p":"Agents: the overview","h":"Where to find agents","t":"","x":"Where to find agents The Agents pane. In the Copilot app, open Agents in the left navigation. Pre-installed agents are listed there. All agents / the Agent Store. All agents opens the Agent Store, a hub for agents from Microsoft, partners and your organisation. Choose Add or Open. Some agents show Request approval instead of Add. @-mentioning. In the Copilot app you can mention an agent in your prompt. Type @ and pick the agent. If that doesn't work for you, use the Agents pane instead. Pinning. Agents can be pinned by Microsoft, by administrators and by you. Researcher and Analyst are pinned by default where you have them. Use the pin icon to keep your favourites at the top. Links. A colleague can send you a link to an agent they built."},{"u":"agents/#chat","p":"Agents: the overview","h":"Which agents you get with Copilot Chat","t":"chat","x":"Which agents you get with Copilot Chat Included: agents grounded in instructions and public websites. Microsoft says these come with Copilot Chat. Microsoft's coaching agents (Idea, Writing, Prompt, Career and Learning Coach) are described as included with Copilot Chat at no extra cost. Your organisation still has to enable them, and a Microsoft setup page suggests some use may be metered where pay-as-you-go is on. Pay-as-you-go: agents that use work data such as SharePoint files and Copilot connectors are billed by usage. They are off by default and your organisation has to set them up. That is a billing setting only an administrator can change. Not available on Chat: knowledge from your email, people data, Teams messages and meetings needs a Copilot licence. Researcher and Analyst: Microsoft's pages put these on the licensed side. See Researcher. How to tell: open the Agents pane. What you see is what your organisation has enabled for you. A missing agent means the licence, or an organisation setting; it is not a fault with your account."},{"u":"agents/#premium","p":"Agents: the overview","h":"Which agents you get with M365 Copilot (Premium)","t":"premium","x":"Which agents you get with M365 Copilot (Premium) Everything in Chat, with no pay-as-you-go step for the work data a licence already covers. Researcher and Analyst: advanced agents that Microsoft lists on the licensed side. See Researcher and Analyst. Agents that use your email, people, Teams messages and meetings as knowledge. Agents you build can use those same sources. See Make your own agent. Your administrator can still switch individual agents off. Researcher and Analyst sit in the core chat experience and may not fall under agent-blocking settings."},{"u":"agents/#section","p":"Agents: the overview","h":"In this section","t":"","x":"In this section Researcher: cited research reports Analyst: from data to insight Other Microsoft agents Make your own agent Using agents safely Agent prompt pack Copilot Studio, Microsoft's separate tool for agents with actions and wider publishing, is outside this guide."},{"u":"researcher/","p":"Researcher","h":"","t":"","x":"Agents · Researcher Researcher Slow on purpose. It does deep research and hands you a cited report."},{"u":"researcher/","p":"Researcher","h":"The short version","t":"","x":"The short version Use it for multi-step research where you want citations. Use ordinary chat for quick things. Say whether you want the web, your work sources, or both, and answer its clarifying questions. Open the citations before you rely on the report."},{"u":"researcher/#when","p":"Researcher","h":"When to use it","t":"","x":"When to use it Researcher handles complex, multi-step research. It draws on the web and, where you have access, your work content (files, emails, meetings and chats you can open) and produces a report with organised sections, cited sources and often visuals. It follows the same permissions as the rest of Copilot. You need… Choose A quick summary, a short reply, light brainstorming Ordinary Copilot chat Deeper reasoning across sources, and a report you can share Researcher Citations and clean structure for a decision Researcher A back-and-forth without heavy analysis Ordinary Copilot chat Researcher deliberately spends longer retrieving and analysing. Microsoft doesn't publish a typical run time, so start it and get on with something else."},{"u":"researcher/#scope","p":"Researcher","h":"Scope it, then answer its questions","t":"","x":"Scope it, then answer its questions Be specific. Clear, detailed questions give more focused results. Define the scope. Say whether you want the web, work sources, or both. Answer the clarifying questions. It may ask first. That step steers the final report."},{"u":"researcher/#read","p":"Researcher","h":"Reading the report","t":"","x":"Reading the report Read the summary, then the sections that matter to your decision. Open the citations for any figure, quote or claim you plan to repeat. Look for what it says it could not verify. Ask for that explicitly in your prompt. Ask a follow-up in the same chat: \"expand section 3\", \"what do the sources disagree on?\""},{"u":"researcher/#chat","p":"Researcher","h":"On Copilot Chat","t":"chat","x":"On Copilot Chat Researcher needs a Copilot licence. Microsoft's Support comparison puts \"advanced agents like Researcher\" on the licensed side and lists the unlicensed side as \"Limited (no advanced agents)\". If you can't see it in the Agents pane, that is the likely reason. For similar help with web research, use ordinary chat with a prompt that asks for sources: Prompt · Chat Research [topic] using public web sources for a decision on [decision]. Give me the key findings, where sources disagree, risks, and a recommendation. Cite every claim with its link. Mark anything you could not verify."},{"u":"researcher/#premium","p":"Researcher","h":"With M365 Copilot (Premium)","t":"premium","x":"With M365 Copilot (Premium) Researcher is listed with the licensed agents. Find it in the Agents pane. If it isn't there, Microsoft says your administrator may not have enabled it. That is an organisation setting. Prompt · Premium Research [topic] for a decision on [decision]. Scope: [the web only / our internal documents and emails only / both]. I need: the key findings, where sources disagree, risks, and a recommendation. Cite every claim. Mark anything you could not verify."},{"u":"analyst/","p":"Analyst","h":"","t":"","x":"Agents · Analyst Analyst Hand it data. Get back trends, charts and an explanation of how it got there."},{"u":"analyst/","p":"Analyst","h":"The short version","t":"","x":"The short version Microsoft describes Analyst as turning raw data into insights and clear visualisations for metrics, trends and forecasts. Always request its working, then check one number by hand. Analyst needs a Copilot licence. On Chat, paste a table into ordinary chat instead."},{"u":"analyst/#what","p":"Analyst","h":"What goes in, and what comes out","t":"","x":"What goes in, and what comes out Give Analyst a data file or table and a question. It answers with findings and visualisations. Microsoft publishes only a short description, with no file-size limits or list of formats, so test with a small, clean sample first."},{"u":"analyst/#check","p":"Analyst","h":"Checking its calculations","t":"","x":"Checking its calculations Request an explanation of how Analyst calculated each figure and which columns it used. Recalculate one total or average yourself. Watch for rows dropped by a filter, and for dates read the wrong way round. Don't forward a chart until you can say in one sentence what it shows."},{"u":"analyst/#vs-excel","p":"Analyst","h":"Analyst or Copilot in Excel?","t":"","x":"Analyst or Copilot in Excel? Choose When Copilot in Excel You want the workbook itself changed or built: formulas, PivotTables, charts that stay in the file. See Excel. Analyst You want questions answered about data and a quick set of insights, without editing a workbook. This split is my rule of thumb, not a Microsoft statement."},{"u":"analyst/#chat","p":"Analyst","h":"On Copilot Chat","t":"chat","x":"On Copilot Chat Microsoft's Support comparison lists Analyst under the advanced agents on the licensed side. If it isn't in your Agents pane, use ordinary chat: paste a small table and ask for trends and a formula, as in the Excel Chat section. Prompt · Chat Here is a table: [paste it]. What are the three biggest trends, and one number that doesn't fit? Explain how you calculated each, and say what you could not tell from this data."},{"u":"analyst/#premium","p":"Analyst","h":"With M365 Copilot (Premium)","t":"premium","x":"With M365 Copilot (Premium) Analyst is pinned by default where you have it. Open it from the Agents pane and attach your file. Prompt · Premium Here is my data: [attach file]. What are the three biggest trends, and one number that doesn't fit? Show a chart for each trend and explain how you calculated it."},{"u":"other-agents/","p":"Other Microsoft agents","h":"","t":"","x":"Agents · Built by Microsoft Other Microsoft agents Besides Researcher and Analyst, Microsoft ships a handful of single-purpose agents."},{"u":"other-agents/","p":"Other Microsoft agents","h":"The short version","t":"","x":"The short version Visual Creator is shown by default. The coaches are added from All agents. Microsoft says \"more agents are coming soon\", so the list changes. Availability varies by subscription and organisation settings."},{"u":"other-agents/#list","p":"Other Microsoft agents","h":"What each one is for","t":"","x":"What each one is for Agent What it does Where Visual Creator Turns descriptions into visuals, prototypes, videos and interactive scenarios Shown by default Idea Coach Generates and refines ideas into structured plans Add via All agents Writing Coach Feedback on tone, grammar and style Add via All agents Prompt Coach Teaches you to structure prompts Add via All agents Learning Coach Helps master subjects and build study plans Add via All agents Career Coach Skills, goals and career planning; Microsoft's page mentions students and institutions Add via All agents Newer items in Microsoft's release notes include a Learning Agent for Windows and a SharePoint list agent. Microsoft doesn't say whether the Learning Agent replaces Learning Coach."},{"u":"other-agents/#prompts","p":"Other Microsoft agents","h":"One prompt each","t":"","x":"One prompt each Visual Creator Create a simple visual that explains [idea] to [audience]. Style: clean and minimal, using [two colours]. Add a short caption. Idea Coach I need ideas for [goal]. Give me five, then help me pick one and turn it into a three-step plan with a first action for this week. Writing Coach Review the text below for tone, grammar and clarity for [audience]. Point out the three changes that would help most, and explain why. [Paste the text here] Prompt Coach Here is a prompt I use: [paste it]. Improve it using goal, context, source and expectations, and explain each change. Learning Coach I want to learn [skill] in [four weeks], at [30 minutes a day]. Build me a study plan with a weekly goal and a short quiz at the end of each week. Career Coach My current role is [role]. I want to move towards [goal]. List the skills I should build, in order, and one small step for each."},{"u":"other-agents/#chat","p":"Other Microsoft agents","h":"On Copilot Chat","t":"chat","x":"On Copilot Chat Microsoft's admin guide says the Idea, Writing, Prompt, Career and Learning coaches are included with Copilot Chat at no additional cost. Your organisation must have enabled the agent for you, and a Microsoft setup page suggests some use may be metered where pay-as-you-go is on. If a coach is in your All agents list, you can use it."},{"u":"other-agents/#premium","p":"Other Microsoft agents","h":"With M365 Copilot (Premium)","t":"premium","x":"With M365 Copilot (Premium) You have the same Microsoft-built agents, plus Researcher and Analyst. Availability still depends on what your administrator has turned on."},{"u":"make-an-agent/","p":"Make your own agent","h":"","t":"","x":"Agents · Build Make your own agent Two ways to build one without code: Agent Builder in the Copilot app, and agents in SharePoint."},{"u":"make-an-agent/","p":"Make your own agent","h":"The short version","t":"","x":"The short version Agent Builder ( New agent in the Copilot app): describe what you want, or fill in the Configure tab yourself. SharePoint agents: create one from a library, list or selected files, scoped to that content. An agent only shows people knowledge they could already open. What you can use as knowledge depends on your tier. See Chat and Premium. Agent Builder can't take actions in other systems. Copilot Studio can, and is outside this guide."},{"u":"make-an-agent/#builder","p":"Make your own agent","h":"Agent Builder: Describe or Configure","t":"","x":"Agent Builder: Describe or Configure Open the Copilot app, choose New agent. Agent Builder isn't available on mobile. You have three ways in: Describe (recommended): chat about what you want. It fills in the name, description, instructions, knowledge and starter prompts for you, and you can see them on the Configure tab. Skip to configure: fill in the fields yourself. Templates. Natural-language Describe works only in the languages Microsoft lists; otherwise use Configure. Do I have it? Look for New agent in your Copilot app. If it is there, you can build. Microsoft's pages differ on whether Chat users get it without pay-as-you-go, so your own app is the answer."},{"u":"make-an-agent/#instructions","p":"Make your own agent","h":"Writing good instructions","t":"","x":"Writing good instructions The Configure tab has these fields, with limits: Field Limit Name 30 characters Description 1,000 characters; also used to decide when to use the agent Instructions 8,000 characters Starter prompts A name and a prompt each; no minimum number A template for instructions that works for most agents: Instruction template Role: You are [role] for [team/audience]. Purpose: Help people with [the jobs it should do]. Use only: [the knowledge sources]. If the answer isn't there, say \"I don't know\" and say who to ask. Format: [short answers / bullets / steps]. Keep a [friendly, plain] tone. Never: [give legal or HR advice / guess / share personal data]. Always: end with the source you used."},{"u":"make-an-agent/#knowledge","p":"Make your own agent","h":"Knowledge sources","t":"","x":"Knowledge sources Knowledge limits per agent: Public websites: 4 (up to two path levels, no query strings) SharePoint files, folders or sites: 100 files; 1 SharePoint list OneDrive files: 50 Files you upload: 20 (not password-protected or certain labelled files) Teams chats: 5 chats and 5 meetings \"Only use specified sources\" prioritises your sources but does not block general AI knowledge. New SharePoint or OneDrive files can show \"Preparing\" for several minutes. Web access is blocked if your organisation turns off web search, even if the toggle looks on."},{"u":"make-an-agent/#test","p":"Make your own agent","h":"Testing and starter prompts","t":"","x":"Testing and starter prompts Use the Try it tab. It becomes available once the agent has a name, description and instructions, and updates as you edit. You can't share prompts, give feedback or @mention other agents in Try it. Test with: Three questions it should answer well. One question the sources can't answer. It should say so. One awkward question that tries to make it break its rules."},{"u":"make-an-agent/#share","p":"Make your own agent","h":"Sharing and permissions","t":"","x":"Sharing and permissions A new agent is private. The share dialog offers Can edit and Can chat. Groups can only be Can chat. You can copy a chat link or an edit link, or turn on org-wide sharing for chat. When it is on, others in your organisation can see the agent in the Agent Store. Who may share is an organisation setting: all users, specific users or groups, or no one. If sharing is restricted, an administrator must approve and deploy the agent. People only see knowledge they can already open. The agent respects each user's permissions and sensitivity labels, and leaves out files they can't see. Sharing an agent does not share the underlying files. Check the file permissions yourself before you share. People you share with need the licence type the agent's capabilities need; otherwise using it may give an error."},{"u":"make-an-agent/#manage","p":"Make your own agent","h":"Editing, updating and deleting","t":"","x":"Editing, updating and deleting Open it from All agents and choose edit. Changes save automatically, but users don't see them until you choose Update, which can take a few minutes. I found no version history or roll-back described. Keep your instructions in a separate document so you can restore them. Deleting is permanent and removes the agent for other owners and shared users. You can add an About this agent description, a privacy statement and terms links."},{"u":"make-an-agent/#sharepoint","p":"Make your own agent","h":"SharePoint agents","t":"","x":"SharePoint agents An agent in SharePoint answers from a library, list, site or selected files. Open the library or list, choose Copilot, then Create an agent. Or select files or folders and right-click Create an agent. Name it, set its scope, and add starter prompts. Editors can add other sites, change branding and edit prompts. Share by copying the link from the chat pane, or through the file's sharing dialog. You need edit permission on the site, plus a Copilot licence or a tenant with pay-as-you-go for SharePoint agents. All site members can create agents. Agents are.agent files in the site's libraries; deleting the file deletes the agent. Default site agent: each site's own Copilot can't be shared. Site owners choose the main agent under Settings > Site AI. Microsoft says upgrades to agents in SharePoint are coming in mid-November 2026, so this may change. After you use a SharePoint agent it appears in your Agents list in the Copilot app, where you can pin it."},{"u":"make-an-agent/#example","p":"Make your own agent","h":"Worked example: a team FAQ agent","t":"","x":"Worked example: a team FAQ agent Collect the answers Put your team's FAQ, onboarding guide and process documents in one SharePoint folder. Check that the whole team can open it. Start in Agent Builder Choose New agent and describe it: \"A friendly team assistant that answers questions about how we work, using only our FAQ folder.\" Add the folder as knowledge On Configure, add the SharePoint folder. Turn on Only use specified sources. Set the instructions Use the template above. Add: \"If it isn't in the folder, say so and name [team owner].\" Add starter prompts \"How do I book leave?\", \"Who approves purchases?\", \"Where is the onboarding checklist?\" Test, then share Run the three tests above. Share with the team as Can chat."},{"u":"make-an-agent/#chat","p":"Make your own agent","h":"What you can use on Copilot Chat","t":"chat","x":"What you can use on Copilot Chat Per Microsoft's licensing table, without usage billing a Copilot Chat agent can use custom instructions, web search, scoped web search, the code interpreter and the image generator. It can't use SharePoint, embedded files or Copilot connectors as knowledge. With pay-as-you-go switched on by your organisation, it adds SharePoint, embedded files and connectors. Email, people data, Teams messages and meetings still need a Copilot licence. So a team FAQ agent that reads a SharePoint folder needs pay-as-you-go on the Chat track. Without it, you can build one grounded in public websites and instructions only. Chat starter You are a plain-English explainer for [team]. Use only the websites listed as knowledge. Answer in five lines or fewer, link the page you used, and say \"I don't know\" if the sites don't say."},{"u":"make-an-agent/#premium","p":"Make your own agent","h":"What you can use with M365 Copilot (Premium)","t":"premium","x":"What you can use with M365 Copilot (Premium) All of the knowledge sources in the licensing table are available: SharePoint, embedded files, connectors, and your email, people data, Teams messages and meetings. Email knowledge is your whole mailbox and can't be narrowed, and the people you share with don't get your email. Be careful that an agent grounded in your mail never becomes a way to reveal it; use SharePoint sources for shared agents. Premium starter You are the project assistant for [project]. Use the SharePoint folder and the project's Teams meetings as knowledge. Answer questions on decisions, owners and next steps. Quote the source, and say when the sources are silent."},{"u":"agents-safely/","p":"Using agents safely","h":"","t":"","x":"Agents · Safety Using agents safely Agents are Copilot with a narrower job. The same rules apply, plus a few of their own."},{"u":"agents-safely/","p":"Using agents safely","h":"The short version","t":"","x":"The short version Agents see only what the person using them could already open. Agents from other companies handle your data under their own privacy statements. Read it first. Never put secrets or personal data in an agent's instructions or knowledge. Retire agents you no longer use."},{"u":"agents-safely/#reach","p":"Using agents safely","h":"What each kind of agent can reach","t":"","x":"What each kind of agent can reach Kind What it can reach Microsoft-built agents Depend on the agent. Researcher uses the web and your work content within your permissions. Agents you or a colleague built Their knowledge sources, filtered by the permissions of whoever is chatting. Agents from partners or other companies Whatever the administrator allowed. Copilot may send them a search query based on your prompt, your activity history and data you can access. Encrypted content can exclude programmatic access, which can stop an agent reading it."},{"u":"agents-safely/#third","p":"Using agents safely","h":"Third-party and organisation agents","t":"","x":"Third-party and organisation agents Check the agent's privacy statement and terms before using it. Microsoft says these determine how your organisation's data is handled. An agent builder can add their own privacy and terms links; the defaults are placeholders, so don't assume they've been filled in. Microsoft's \"no training\" commitments are about its own service. Don't assume they cover an agent built by another company. Administrators decide which agents are allowed and can see their permissions. That is an organisation setting."},{"u":"agents-safely/#dont","p":"Using agents safely","h":"What not to put in instructions or knowledge","t":"","x":"What not to put in instructions or knowledge Passwords, keys, tokens or personal data. Files you wouldn't want every user of the agent to see answers from. Anyone who can use the agent can see answers grounded in embedded files. Whole mailboxes in a shared agent. Anything your organisation classes as restricted."},{"u":"agents-safely/#check","p":"Using agents safely","h":"Checking outputs","t":"","x":"Checking outputs Ask for sources, then open them. Test an agent with a question its sources can't answer. It should say so. Treat answers about people, money and customers as drafts for a human to check."},{"u":"agents-safely/#retire","p":"Using agents safely","h":"Retiring agents you no longer use","t":"","x":"Retiring agents you no longer use Remove people you shared it with. Removing someone from an agent does not remove their access to its files, so fix the file permissions too. Delete the agent. For Agent Builder agents this is permanent. For SharePoint agents, delete the.agent file. Unpin agents you no longer use from your Agents list."},{"u":"agents-safely/#chat","p":"Using agents safely","h":"On Copilot Chat","t":"chat","x":"On Copilot Chat Agents with work data are off by default and billed by use. An administrator turns them on. If an agent says you can't use it, that is most likely a setting or a billing choice, not something you did."},{"u":"agents-safely/#premium","p":"Using agents safely","h":"With M365 Copilot (Premium)","t":"premium","x":"With M365 Copilot (Premium) An agent built on your mailbox or meetings can surface sensitive material. Build shared agents on SharePoint sources, not on your email. Also read Safety and privacy."},{"u":"agent-prompts/","p":"Agent prompt pack","h":"","t":"","x":"Agents · Prompt pack Agent prompt pack Copy, change the brackets, paste. About these prompts Results differ by agent and licence. Treat each one as a starting point and adjust it."},{"u":"agent-prompts/#builtin","p":"Agent prompt pack","h":"Prompts for the built-in agents","t":"","x":"Prompts for the built-in agents Researcher Prompt Research [topic] for a decision on [decision]. Scope: [web only / work sources / both]. Give key findings, where sources disagree, risks and a recommendation. Cite every claim and mark anything unverified. Analyst Prompt Here is my data: [attach file]. What are the three biggest trends, and one number that doesn't fit? Show a chart for each and explain the calculation. Visual Creator, and the coaches See the prompts on Other Microsoft agents. Researcher and Analyst are licensed agents; see the overview. On the Chat track, use the Chat variants on their pages."},{"u":"agent-prompts/#templates","p":"Agent prompt pack","h":"Instruction templates for custom agents","t":"","x":"Instruction templates for custom agents Paste into the Instructions field of Agent Builder. See Make your own agent. Team FAQ agent Instructions You are the team assistant for [team]. Answer questions about how we work using only the knowledge sources. Keep answers to five lines. Name the document you used. If it isn't covered, say \"I don't know\" and name [person] to ask. Onboarding buddy Instructions You help new starters in [team] in their first 30 days. Use only the onboarding documents. Answer in plain words, give steps in order, and finish each answer with \"What would you like to know next?\" Never give HR, legal or pay advice; point to [person] instead. Writing-style checker Instructions You check text against our style guide (in the knowledge source). For any text I paste, list up to five changes that would bring it in line, quote the rule for each, then give a corrected version. Do not change facts, figures or names. Meeting prep Instructions You prepare me for meetings. When I give a meeting topic and attendees, produce: a one-line goal, a timed agenda, three questions to ask, and two likely objections. Use only the project documents in the knowledge source. Say what is missing. Policy explainer Instructions You explain our policies in plain English using only the policy documents in the knowledge source. Give the short answer first, then the policy wording you relied on and its document name. You are not a decision-maker. For exceptions or doubt, say who to ask. Never guess."},{"u":"safety/","p":"Safety and privacy","h":"","t":"","x":"Guide Safety and privacy The honest version of \"is it safe?\". What is protected, what is not, and what is up to you."},{"u":"safety/","p":"Safety and privacy","h":"The short version","t":"","x":"The short version Copilot shows you only what you could already open. It does not break permissions. Microsoft says your prompts, responses and work data are not used to train its foundation models. Your chats are stored, and your employer's administrators can search them. The real risk is files that are shared too widely in your company, and answers that are wrong."},{"u":"safety/#sees","p":"Safety and privacy","h":"What Copilot can see","t":"","x":"What Copilot can see Copilot reaches your work data through Microsoft Graph and surfaces only items you have at least view permission for. That includes people outside your company who have been given access through collaboration features such as shared channels in Teams. It also honours sensitivity labels and encryption that your company applies. If a file's usage rights stop you from using it, they stop Copilot too. On the Chat track Copilot cannot search your emails and files for you, so exposure comes from what you paste, upload or open yourself. On the Premium track Copilot searches Graph for you, so anything you can open can turn up in an answer. That makes the next section matter more."},{"u":"safety/#overshare","p":"Safety and privacy","h":"The real risk: oversharing","t":"","x":"The real risk: oversharing Copilot does not give you extra access. But it makes content you already can reach much easier to find. A file in a SharePoint site that \"everyone\" can open was always technically visible to you; Copilot can now surface it from a plain question. Microsoft's own advice for companies is to tighten permissions, and it offers tools such as SharePoint Advanced Management for reducing oversharing. Two things you can do: Share files with specific people, not \"anyone with the link\" or whole-company groups. If Copilot shows you something that you think you should not see, don't use it. Report it through your organisation's route instead."},{"u":"safety/#kept","p":"Safety and privacy","h":"What is stored, and who can see it","t":"","x":"What is stored, and who can see it Your prompts, Copilot's responses and the citations are stored as your Copilot activity history. It is encrypted and handled under the same commitments as your company's other Microsoft 365 content. Administrators can search and set retention for that history using tools such as Content search and Microsoft Purview. Assume a prompt is as visible to your employer as an email. Memory works the same way: what Copilot remembers about you is stored in your mailbox and administrators can search it. You can see and delete it yourself. See Power features. You can delete your own activity history in the My Account portal ( myaccount.microsoft.com)."},{"u":"safety/#training","p":"Safety and privacy","h":"What Microsoft says it does not do","t":"","x":"What Microsoft says it does not do Prompts, responses and data accessed through Microsoft Graph are not used to train the foundation models. Copilot services have opted out of human abuse-monitoring review. Copilot is restricted from making judgments about an employee's performance, attitude, emotional state or personal characteristics from their workplace communication. These are Microsoft's statements about its commercial service when you sign in with a work account. Personal-account Copilot has separate terms."},{"u":"safety/#dont","p":"Safety and privacy","h":"What not to paste","t":"","x":"What not to paste Check your company policy first Technical protections do not replace your employer's rules. Find out whether you have any, then apply them. Anything your company classifies as restricted, or that a legal, HR or security rule says must not leave a system. Other people's personal details you have no reason to process, such as health, performance or disciplinary information. Passwords, keys and access tokens. Anything into the personal version of Copilot. If you do not see Work and the green shield, you are not in the protected experience."},{"u":"safety/#wrong","p":"Safety and privacy","h":"Wrong answers","t":"","x":"Wrong answers Microsoft says outputs are not guaranteed to be fully factual and that you should use your judgement before sharing them. Treat Copilot like a fast colleague who sometimes confidently gets it wrong: Check names, numbers, dates and quotes against the source. Request a citation for each claim, then open the citation. Be most careful with meeting summaries and anything that goes to a client."},{"u":"safety/#web","p":"Safety and privacy","h":"Web search and agents","t":"","x":"Web search and agents When Copilot searches the web it turns parts of your prompt into a query sent to Bing. Don't include things in a prompt that you would not type into a search engine. Agents built by other companies have their own privacy statements. Your admin decides which are allowed. If you are given one, read how it handles your data."},{"u":"safety/#ask","p":"Safety and privacy","h":"Before you paste: a self-check","t":"","x":"Before you paste: a self-check Am I in the work version? Look for Work and the green shield. Would I be comfortable if this prompt were read by my manager or a colleague in compliance? Does it contain a password, key, customer detail or someone's personal data I have no reason to process? Have I looked for my organisation's guidance? Look for a View info from your organization link in the app. Is the file I am about to share with Copilot shared only with the people who should see it? Check its sharing settings."},{"u":"troubleshooting/","p":"When it breaks","h":"","t":"","x":"Guide Fix it Copilot not showing up, not seeing your files, or not answering. Start with the first one. It is the cause more often than not."},{"u":"troubleshooting/","p":"When it breaks","h":"The short version","t":"","x":"The short version Most problems are the account (personal instead of work) or the licence (the thing you expected isn't included in yours). Steps marked Microsoft come from Microsoft's documentation. Steps marked Reported are commonly suggested elsewhere but are not in Microsoft's pages."},{"u":"troubleshooting/#pick","p":"When it breaks","h":"What's wrong?","t":"","x":"What's wrong? Not sure I'm in the right Copilot Work or personal account, and your label. No Copilot button in Word, Excel or PowerPoint Label, licence, updates. It says access is turned off Organisation settings and networks. It can't find my files, emails or meetings What each licence can reach. It invented something How to pin it to the source. Copilot in my Teams meeting isn't working Organiser settings and transcripts."},{"u":"troubleshooting/#account","p":"When it breaks","h":"\"I'm not sure I'm in the right Copilot\"","t":"","x":"\"I'm not sure I'm in the right Copilot\" Microsoft Work and personal accounts keep separate chats, files, and protections. In the work version you should see Work under your profile and a green data protection shield. Sign out, then sign in at copilot.cloud.microsoft with your work email. Check the label under your name: Copilot Chat (Basic), M365 Copilot (Basic) or M365 Copilot (Premium). In Edge, make sure the browser profile itself is signed in with your work account."},{"u":"troubleshooting/#missing","p":"When it breaks","h":"\"There's no Copilot button in Word, Excel or PowerPoint\"","t":"","x":"\"There's no Copilot button in Word, Excel or PowerPoint\" Work down this list: Check your label. Copilot Chat (Basic) does not include Copilot inside Word, Excel, PowerPoint or OneNote. M365 Copilot (Basic) has standard access, subject to capacity. Know the April 2026 report. Several university help pages say embedded Copilot in these apps ended for people without a paid licence around then. Microsoft's current pages match that for the Copilot Chat (Basic) label. If you lost it recently, check your label and your update channel first (below) before spending time on fixes. Check your licence yourself. Microsoft publishes a licence details page: Copilot License Details. Reported In the app, open File > Account and look for Update License, then restart the app. The button is not always there. Reported Make sure the app is up to date. Some features are reported to be missing on the Semi-Annual Enterprise Channel; your update channel is shown under File > Account. An admin setting can switch it off Microsoft If your company turns off \"connected experiences that analyze your content\", Copilot is unavailable in Word, Excel, OneNote, Outlook and PowerPoint, regardless of licence."},{"u":"troubleshooting/#blocked","p":"When it breaks","h":"\"It says access is turned off\"","t":"","x":"\"It says access is turned off\" Microsoft Administrators can block Copilot Chat for everyone or for chosen groups, including people with a paid licence. You may still see Chat in the app menu, but selecting it shows a message that access is disabled by policy. Your company can add a link there to its own guidance, titled View info from your organization. Follow that link. Microsoft If the app won't load at all, part of *.cloud.microsoft may be blocked on your network. Microsoft says all of that domain needs to be allowed. That is a network setting, not something you can fix. Try a different network, such as a phone hotspot, to see whether that is the cause."},{"u":"troubleshooting/#files","p":"When it breaks","h":"\"It can't find my files, emails or meetings\"","t":"","x":"\"It can't find my files, emails or meetings\" Chat can't search your work data. Upload or paste the content, or open it in Outlook or Teams first. This isn't a fault. It can. If it still can't see something, check that you have permission to open that file yourself. Copilot only shows what you can access. Work IQ is an organisation setting. When it is off, answers aren't grounded in your work data. You can't switch it on, but you can recognise it: answers about files you can open yourself come back with no work context."},{"u":"troubleshooting/#wrong","p":"When it breaks","h":"\"It invented something\"","t":"","x":"\"It invented something\" Ask: \"Where does that come from? Quote the source.\" Then open the citation. Re-run with an instruction such as \"use only the attached document; if it isn't there, say so\". Run it a second time. Microsoft notes results vary from run to run. If it is wrong about something that matters, don't send it. Fix it by hand."},{"u":"troubleshooting/#teams","p":"When it breaks","h":"\"Copilot in my Teams meeting isn't working\"","t":"","x":"\"Copilot in my Teams meeting isn't working\" These come from Microsoft's page on Copilot in Teams meetings. Symptom Likely reason Can't ask questions after the meeting The organiser must have chosen \"during and after the meeting\", which needs transcription. Without a transcript, post-meeting questions about what was said don't work. Copilot is missing entirely The organiser set Copilot to Off, which also disables recording and transcription. Only the organiser can change it (Online meeting options > Copilot and other AI). It doesn't work in this meeting It doesn't work in meetings hosted by another organisation. No \"intelligent recap\" Recap needs a Teams Premium add-on or a Copilot licence. Can't export an answer to Word or Excel A sensitivity label or a \"turn off copying and forwarding\" meeting option can block it. Joined late If you join more than 5 minutes after the start and Copilot is on, it offers a catch-up summary."},{"u":"troubleshooting/#escalate","p":"When it breaks","h":"Note these details if you need help","t":"","x":"Note these details if you need help If you need to ask for help, these details get you a faster answer. Copy this into a note and fill it in: Your notes - App: [Word / Excel / Teams / copilot.cloud.microsoft] - What I expected: [e.g. a Copilot button in Word] - What happens: [e.g. no button / \"access is turned off\"] - Label under my name at copilot.cloud.microsoft: [Copilot Chat (Basic) / M365 Copilot (Basic) / M365 Copilot (Premium) / none] - Account I'm signed in with: [work email] - Since when: [date, or \"never worked\"]"}];